1
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,793
Hey, what are you doing?

2
00:06:58,877 --> 00:07:01,296
You're supposed to be
upstairs sleeping.

3
00:07:01,379 --> 00:07:04,007
Stanley keeps waking me up.

4
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:07,135
Oh, ho, ho. I get it.

5
00:07:07,218 --> 00:07:09,095
Boy, are you getting heavy.

6
00:07:09,179 --> 00:07:12,098
Don't let her pull more
than her usual nonsense.

7
00:07:12,182 --> 00:07:14,642
Two stories,
two glasses of water.

8
00:07:14,726 --> 00:07:17,604
Jack, you're going
to miss the plane.

9
00:07:17,687 --> 00:07:20,106
OK. Cricket,
you listen to me.

10
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:23,651
Go upstairs with Mrs. Wheeler now
and go to sleep.

11
00:07:23,735 --> 00:07:25,111
On my business trip,

12
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:27,113
I'll get Stanley
a little brother.

13
00:07:27,197 --> 00:07:28,656
Will you promise?

14
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:29,949
I promise.

15
00:08:03,149 --> 00:08:04,859
Can I get you anything, sir?

16
00:08:05,985 --> 00:08:07,737
No, thank you very much.

17
00:08:07,821 --> 00:08:10,281
If you try and get sleep,

18
00:08:10,323 --> 00:08:12,200
the flight will go faster.

19
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:15,203
I can never sleep on a plane.

20
00:08:15,286 --> 00:08:16,621
Turbulence.

21
00:08:16,663 --> 00:08:17,997
Pardon?

22
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:20,041
Turbulence...

23
00:08:20,125 --> 00:08:22,043
Solar radiation
heats the Earth's crust,

24
00:08:22,127 --> 00:08:24,045
warm air rises,
cold airs descends.

25
00:08:24,129 --> 00:08:26,381
Turbulence.
I don't like that.

26
00:08:26,464 --> 00:08:28,675
Oh. Well, try to sleep anyway.

27
00:09:06,713 --> 00:09:08,006
Dr Ryan?

28
00:09:25,065 --> 00:09:28,151
Jack, boy,
get yourself in here.

29
00:09:28,234 --> 00:09:30,195
Jesus!
You look like hell.

30
00:09:30,236 --> 00:09:32,197
Thank you very much, Admiral.

31
00:09:32,238 --> 00:09:33,365
Want coffee?

32
00:09:33,406 --> 00:09:35,325
I think I need some coffee.

33
00:09:35,408 --> 00:09:38,828
I'm not going to ask you
how your flight went.

34
00:09:38,912 --> 00:09:41,831
When's the last time
you got some sleep?

35
00:09:41,915 --> 00:09:43,333
Oh, I don't know.

36
00:09:43,416 --> 00:09:45,835
This thing's still
on London time.

37
00:09:45,919 --> 00:09:47,337
Milk and sugar?

38
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:48,880
Milk and sugar.

39
00:09:48,922 --> 00:09:50,340
So how's Caroline?

40
00:09:50,423 --> 00:09:52,342
Last time I saw her

41
00:09:52,425 --> 00:09:55,845
was at your granddad's place
in Maine, wasn't it?

42
00:09:55,929 --> 00:09:58,723
She's fine.
Sends you her best.

43
00:09:58,765 --> 00:10:00,100
And Sally?

44
00:10:00,183 --> 00:10:01,351
She's fine.

45
00:10:01,434 --> 00:10:04,437
What is she now, 3?

46
00:10:04,521 --> 00:10:08,149
Ah, no, she's
a very precocious 5.

47
00:10:08,233 --> 00:10:11,736
She announced to Caroline
and I the other day

48
00:10:11,778 --> 00:10:14,698
that her life would be
considerably less lonely

49
00:10:14,781 --> 00:10:18,201
if we were to buy her
a baby brother.

50
00:10:18,284 --> 00:10:20,704
But she decided
it would be enough

51
00:10:20,787 --> 00:10:23,707
if we were to
buy one for Stanley.

52
00:10:24,874 --> 00:10:26,126
Who's Stanley?

53
00:10:26,209 --> 00:10:28,461
Uh... Stanley's a bear.

54
00:10:31,297 --> 00:10:32,465
All right.

55
00:10:32,549 --> 00:10:35,218
What's important enough
to get you on a plane

56
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:36,886
in the middle of the night?

57
00:10:36,970 --> 00:10:40,181
British lntelligence obtained
these pictures two days ago.

58
00:10:40,265 --> 00:10:41,683
She's the Red October,

59
00:10:41,766 --> 00:10:43,518
the latest typhoon class.

60
00:10:43,601 --> 00:10:45,520
Big son of a bitch.

61
00:10:45,603 --> 00:10:48,231
12 meters longer than
the standard typhoon.

62
00:10:48,314 --> 00:10:49,691
3 meters wider.

63
00:10:49,774 --> 00:10:51,818
The captain's name is Ramius.

64
00:10:51,901 --> 00:10:52,861
One of yours?

65
00:10:52,944 --> 00:10:54,988
Yeah. I did his bio last year.

66
00:10:55,071 --> 00:10:56,990
He's taken out the lead boat

67
00:10:57,073 --> 00:10:59,993
in each new sub class
for 10 years.

68
00:11:00,076 --> 00:11:01,494
Fairly good
political connections.

69
00:11:01,578 --> 00:11:04,497
Trained most of their
attack boat skippers.

70
00:11:04,581 --> 00:11:07,792
The Russians call him
Vilnius Nastavnic...

71
00:11:07,834 --> 00:11:11,421
the Vilnius schoolmaster.

72
00:11:12,505 --> 00:11:13,923
What are these doors?

73
00:11:14,007 --> 00:11:17,969
Those doors, sir, are the problem.

74
00:11:18,011 --> 00:11:19,262
I don't know what they are.

75
00:11:19,346 --> 00:11:20,764
Neither do the British.

76
00:11:20,847 --> 00:11:22,265
Perhaps our friends in Murmansk

77
00:11:22,349 --> 00:11:24,267
have come up with something new.

78
00:11:24,351 --> 00:11:27,771
With your permission,
I'd like to show these to someone.

79
00:11:27,854 --> 00:11:28,980
Do you know Skip Tyler?

80
00:11:29,022 --> 00:11:30,190
Sub driver.

81
00:11:30,273 --> 00:11:33,318
Was. Clipped by a drunk driver.
Lost his leg.

82
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,362
He's teaching at the academy,

83
00:11:35,403 --> 00:11:36,571
consulting for Navy labs.

84
00:11:36,654 --> 00:11:37,697
Clearance?

85
00:11:37,781 --> 00:11:39,366
Top secret or better.

86
00:11:40,408 --> 00:11:42,994
Margie, have a car for Dr Ryan

87
00:11:43,036 --> 00:11:45,038
downstairs in 10 minutes.

88
00:11:49,125 --> 00:11:51,044
A satellite caught Red October

89
00:11:51,086 --> 00:11:53,338
in Polijarny lnlet this morning.

90
00:12:34,045 --> 00:12:35,964
Do you hear it?

91
00:12:37,173 --> 00:12:38,425
No.

92
00:12:38,508 --> 00:12:39,718
Beaumont, at Cal Tech

93
00:12:39,759 --> 00:12:42,429
we used to do this
in our sleep.

94
00:12:42,512 --> 00:12:44,139
You hear it now?

95
00:12:44,222 --> 00:12:45,682
Wait a minute.

96
00:12:45,765 --> 00:12:46,891
Maybe.

97
00:12:46,933 --> 00:12:47,934
Um...

98
00:12:48,018 --> 00:12:49,978
It's buried in surface clutter?

99
00:12:50,061 --> 00:12:51,604
Yes.

100
00:12:51,688 --> 00:12:53,648
I should go to SAPS?

101
00:12:53,732 --> 00:12:55,650
Correct, Seaman Beaumont.

102
00:12:55,734 --> 00:12:58,153
Signal algorithmic
processing systems.

103
00:12:58,236 --> 00:12:59,654
Give it a week,

104
00:12:59,738 --> 00:13:02,323
and you'll be
teaching at Cal Tech.

105
00:13:03,867 --> 00:13:05,160
So...

106
00:13:05,243 --> 00:13:09,247
Iike Beethoven on the computer,

107
00:13:09,289 --> 00:13:11,583
you have labored to produce...

108
00:13:18,048 --> 00:13:19,674
a biologic.

109
00:13:21,718 --> 00:13:22,886
A what?

110
00:13:22,969 --> 00:13:25,430
A whale, Beaumont.
A whale...

111
00:13:25,472 --> 00:13:26,848
A marine mammal

112
00:13:26,931 --> 00:13:30,268
that knows a hell
of a lot more about sonar

113
00:13:30,352 --> 00:13:31,686
than you do.

114
00:13:31,770 --> 00:13:33,146
Kill the SAPS.

115
00:13:33,188 --> 00:13:36,900
Train over to 2-6-9
and let's try it again.

116
00:13:36,983 --> 00:13:39,444
If he gets to ragging
on you too bad,

117
00:13:39,527 --> 00:13:41,946
ask him about Pavarotti.

118
00:13:41,988 --> 00:13:43,907
We don't have time
for sea stories.

119
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:46,951
I was in the middle
of teaching Seaman Beaumont

120
00:13:47,035 --> 00:13:48,995
the intricacies
of modern sonar. Now...

121
00:13:49,037 --> 00:13:51,289
I ain't chief of the boat.

122
00:13:51,331 --> 00:13:53,124
I'm Sheena,
Queen of the Jungle.

123
00:13:53,208 --> 00:13:54,292
Cob, please.

124
00:13:54,334 --> 00:13:55,627
Come on, Cob. Tell me.

125
00:13:55,669 --> 00:13:58,380
OK. Fine. Go ahead.

126
00:13:58,463 --> 00:14:00,465
Ahem... Seaman Jones here,

127
00:14:00,548 --> 00:14:02,884
he's into music in a big way.

128
00:14:02,967 --> 00:14:04,886
He figures this whole boat

129
00:14:04,969 --> 00:14:07,305
is basically just his own

130
00:14:07,347 --> 00:14:10,183
personal, private stereo set.

131
00:14:10,266 --> 00:14:12,143
He gets this
piece of Pavarotti...

132
00:14:12,185 --> 00:14:13,186
Paganini.

133
00:14:13,269 --> 00:14:14,187
Whatever.

134
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:15,188
Paganini.

135
00:14:15,271 --> 00:14:16,690
This is my story, OK?

136
00:14:16,731 --> 00:14:19,651
Tell it right.
Pavarotti's a tenor.

137
00:14:19,693 --> 00:14:21,236
Paganini was a composer.

138
00:14:21,319 --> 00:14:23,738
OK. So he's got this music,

139
00:14:23,822 --> 00:14:26,783
and he's got it out
in the water.

140
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:28,785
He's listening
on his headsets,

141
00:14:28,868 --> 00:14:30,161
happy as a clam.

142
00:14:30,203 --> 00:14:31,454
Then all hell breaks loose.

143
00:14:31,538 --> 00:14:33,915
A slew of boats
out of San Diego...

144
00:14:33,998 --> 00:14:35,458
Including one
way out at Pearl.

145
00:14:35,542 --> 00:14:37,502
Including one
way out at Pearl.

146
00:14:37,544 --> 00:14:39,629
All of a sudden,
they start hearing...

147
00:14:39,713 --> 00:14:40,755
- Pavarotti.
- Pavarotti...

148
00:14:40,839 --> 00:14:43,174
coming out of their asses.

149
00:14:44,968 --> 00:14:47,178
What do I do?

150
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:48,847
I got it.

151
00:14:51,349 --> 00:14:54,227
Conn. Sonar.
New contact, bearing 0-9-7.

152
00:14:54,310 --> 00:14:56,730
Designate contact
number Sierra 3-5.

153
00:14:56,813 --> 00:14:58,732
Captain, aye.
What've you got, Jones?

154
00:14:58,815 --> 00:15:00,275
Distant contact,
probably submerged.

155
00:15:00,358 --> 00:15:01,735
It's a wild guess,

156
00:15:01,818 --> 00:15:04,279
but I'd say we hit a boomer

157
00:15:04,362 --> 00:15:05,739
coming out of the barn.

158
00:15:05,822 --> 00:15:08,575
Could be a missile boat
out of Polijarny.

159
00:15:08,658 --> 00:15:09,993
OK. Start your track.

160
00:15:10,076 --> 00:15:11,828
I'll be there in a minute.

161
00:15:11,911 --> 00:15:13,705
Sonar, aye.

162
00:17:26,463 --> 00:17:28,631
...Armageddon.

163
00:17:28,715 --> 00:17:31,134
And the seventh angel
poured forth his bowl

164
00:17:31,217 --> 00:17:32,635
into the air,

165
00:17:32,719 --> 00:17:35,138
and a voice cried out
from heaven, saying,

166
00:17:35,221 --> 00:17:37,057
"It is done".

167
00:17:39,184 --> 00:17:41,102
A man with your responsibilities

168
00:17:41,186 --> 00:17:43,813
reading about the end
of the world. Huh.

169
00:17:43,897 --> 00:17:45,273
And what's this?

170
00:17:45,357 --> 00:17:47,734
"I am become death,

171
00:17:47,817 --> 00:17:50,737
the destroyer of worlds".

172
00:17:50,820 --> 00:17:53,239
It is an ancient Hindu text

173
00:17:53,323 --> 00:17:54,783
quoted by an American.

174
00:17:54,866 --> 00:17:55,825
American?

175
00:17:55,909 --> 00:17:58,286
Mmm.
He invented the atomic bomb,

176
00:17:58,370 --> 00:18:01,081
and he was later accused
of being a Communist.

177
00:18:01,164 --> 00:18:04,542
You wrote and underlined
these passages?

178
00:18:06,211 --> 00:18:07,295
No.

179
00:18:10,256 --> 00:18:12,634
This book belonged to my wife.

180
00:18:12,717 --> 00:18:14,678
I keep it
for sentimental value.

181
00:18:14,761 --> 00:18:16,638
I'm sorry, comrade Captain.

182
00:18:16,721 --> 00:18:18,640
Your wife was...
a beautiful woman.

183
00:18:18,723 --> 00:18:20,684
Her death was... unfortunate.

184
00:18:20,767 --> 00:18:22,727
I'm only doing my job.

185
00:18:22,769 --> 00:18:24,396
It is my responsibility.

186
00:18:27,273 --> 00:18:31,069
How many agents did the KGB
put aboard my boat?

187
00:18:31,152 --> 00:18:32,612
Your boat, Captain?

188
00:18:32,696 --> 00:18:33,947
Yes.

189
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:37,409
This vessel belongs to
the people of the Soviet Union.

190
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:41,788
Besides, if the KGB or the GRU
has agents aboard,

191
00:18:41,871 --> 00:18:44,916
I will be the last to know.

192
00:18:46,626 --> 00:18:49,754
I suggest we open
our orders, Captain.

193
00:18:51,172 --> 00:18:52,757
Certainly, comrade.

194
00:19:35,717 --> 00:19:37,385
"From commander...

195
00:19:37,469 --> 00:19:39,095
strategic submarine forces

196
00:19:39,179 --> 00:19:41,139
red banner northern fleet".

197
00:19:41,222 --> 00:19:46,561
We're to proceed north
to grid square reference 54-90

198
00:19:46,644 --> 00:19:50,482
and rendezvous with
alfa submarine Konovalov.

199
00:19:50,523 --> 00:19:52,359
Captain Tupolev's boat.

200
00:19:52,442 --> 00:19:53,902
You know Tupolev?

201
00:19:53,985 --> 00:19:56,404
I know he descends
from aristocracy

202
00:19:56,488 --> 00:19:59,616
and that he was your student.

203
00:19:59,699 --> 00:20:01,534
It's rumored he has
a special place

204
00:20:01,618 --> 00:20:02,994
in his heart for you.

205
00:20:03,078 --> 00:20:06,831
There's little room in Tupolev's heart
for anyone but Tupolev.

206
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:14,589
Having made contact,
we are to run a series of drills.

207
00:20:14,673 --> 00:20:17,634
Tupolev will hunt us
while we test our ship.

208
00:20:17,717 --> 00:20:19,219
Having evaluated
the operational readiness

209
00:20:19,260 --> 00:20:20,720
of the caterpillar drive,

210
00:20:20,762 --> 00:20:22,722
we are to return to Polijarny

211
00:20:22,764 --> 00:20:25,308
on or about the 16th
of this month.

212
00:20:26,810 --> 00:20:29,270
Captain, this is
an historic moment.

213
00:20:29,354 --> 00:20:31,231
I'd like to make a request.

214
00:20:31,314 --> 00:20:32,774
Before you proceed, comrade...

215
00:20:32,857 --> 00:20:34,401
I know it's not protocol,

216
00:20:34,484 --> 00:20:36,569
but would you permit me
to post our orders

217
00:20:36,653 --> 00:20:39,239
and inform the crew
of our mission?

218
00:20:45,286 --> 00:20:46,371
Aah!

219
00:21:04,222 --> 00:21:05,890
Where I'm going...

220
00:21:05,932 --> 00:21:08,059
you cannot follow.

221
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:40,550
Dr Petrov, report
to my cabin immediately.

222
00:21:40,633 --> 00:21:42,677
There's been
a dreadful accident.

223
00:22:12,874 --> 00:22:13,958
Hey, buddy!

224
00:22:23,176 --> 00:22:25,428
Daniel, got any more rods?

225
00:22:25,512 --> 00:22:26,638
More rods!

226
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:29,391
Listen to me.
There's no cradle underneath it.

227
00:22:29,474 --> 00:22:30,850
Get it over.

228
00:22:30,934 --> 00:22:32,852
Relax!
Stop being an old maid.

229
00:22:32,936 --> 00:22:35,855
You want to come up here
and do it yourself?

230
00:22:35,939 --> 00:22:37,357
All right. Bring her up.

231
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:38,525
What? What?

232
00:22:39,818 --> 00:22:41,027
Ryan!

233
00:22:41,111 --> 00:22:42,529
Who let you in here?

234
00:22:42,612 --> 00:22:43,780
Hey, Skip,

235
00:22:43,863 --> 00:22:46,700
what, have they got you
playing with models now?

236
00:22:46,783 --> 00:22:48,034
Ahh!

237
00:22:48,118 --> 00:22:49,494
Damn it, Bill!

238
00:22:49,577 --> 00:22:51,579
Tell them to slow down!

239
00:22:51,663 --> 00:22:53,581
I'm doing it.
I'm doing it.

240
00:22:53,665 --> 00:22:55,083
Calm down, will you?

241
00:22:55,166 --> 00:22:58,003
It's not a model. The DSRV.

242
00:22:58,086 --> 00:22:59,045
Rescue sub.

243
00:22:59,129 --> 00:23:00,547
I know what it is.

244
00:23:00,630 --> 00:23:02,757
What are you doing with it?

245
00:23:02,841 --> 00:23:05,260
We're rigging it
with a generic docking collar

246
00:23:05,343 --> 00:23:07,262
so it'll mate with
British, German...

247
00:23:07,345 --> 00:23:08,680
just about anybody's sub.

248
00:23:08,722 --> 00:23:11,057
We can get it
anywhere in the world

249
00:23:11,141 --> 00:23:12,517
in 24 hours.

250
00:23:13,893 --> 00:23:15,353
How's your back?

251
00:23:15,437 --> 00:23:17,439
It's fine.

252
00:23:17,522 --> 00:23:19,983
Have you got a minute?

253
00:23:21,359 --> 00:23:23,778
Bigger than
a regular typhoon.

254
00:23:24,863 --> 00:23:26,322
What are these doors?

255
00:23:26,406 --> 00:23:28,783
You don't miss much, do you?

256
00:23:28,867 --> 00:23:31,953
Those are too big
to be torpedo tubes.

257
00:23:33,413 --> 00:23:35,623
Would you launch
an ICBM horizontally?

258
00:23:35,707 --> 00:23:37,667
Sure. Why would you want to?

259
00:23:37,751 --> 00:23:39,878
They're symmetrical.

260
00:23:41,004 --> 00:23:43,506
Right down
the long axis of the sub.

261
00:23:43,590 --> 00:23:46,301
How about a towed sonar array?

262
00:23:46,384 --> 00:23:48,970
Nope.
Too close to the screws.

263
00:23:49,054 --> 00:23:50,263
I'll be...

264
00:23:52,932 --> 00:23:53,892
This...

265
00:23:53,933 --> 00:23:55,435
This could be a caterpillar.

266
00:23:55,477 --> 00:23:56,478
A what?

267
00:23:56,561 --> 00:23:58,229
Uh, a caterpillar drive.

268
00:23:58,271 --> 00:24:00,690
Magneto-hydrodynamic propulsion.

269
00:24:00,774 --> 00:24:02,067
You follow?

270
00:24:02,108 --> 00:24:03,193
No.

271
00:24:03,276 --> 00:24:06,654
It's like a...
a jet engine for the water.

272
00:24:06,738 --> 00:24:08,865
Goes in the front,
gets squirted out the back.

273
00:24:08,948 --> 00:24:10,867
Only it's got no moving parts,

274
00:24:10,950 --> 00:24:12,827
so it's very, very quiet.

275
00:24:12,911 --> 00:24:15,163
Like how quiet?

276
00:24:15,246 --> 00:24:18,208
It's doubtful our sonar
would even pick it up.

277
00:24:18,291 --> 00:24:19,668
And if it did,

278
00:24:19,751 --> 00:24:21,670
it would sound
like whales humping

279
00:24:21,753 --> 00:24:23,213
or a seismic anomaly.

280
00:24:23,296 --> 00:24:24,714
Anything but a submarine.

281
00:24:24,798 --> 00:24:27,926
We messed with this
a couple of years ago.

282
00:24:28,009 --> 00:24:29,719
Couldn't make it work.

283
00:24:30,679 --> 00:24:32,138
They really built this?

284
00:24:32,222 --> 00:24:34,849
This isn't a mock-up
or anything?

285
00:24:34,933 --> 00:24:37,310
She put to sea this morning.

286
00:24:43,066 --> 00:24:44,442
When I was 12,

287
00:24:44,526 --> 00:24:47,987
I helped my daddy build
a bomb shelter in our basement

288
00:24:48,029 --> 00:24:50,448
because some fool parked
a dozen warheads

289
00:24:50,532 --> 00:24:54,035
90 miles off the
coast of Florida.

290
00:24:54,119 --> 00:24:57,038
This thing could park
a couple of hundred warheads

291
00:24:57,122 --> 00:24:58,540
off Washington and New York,

292
00:24:58,623 --> 00:25:01,042
and no one would know
anything about it

293
00:25:01,126 --> 00:25:02,877
until it was all over.

294
00:25:14,389 --> 00:25:16,891
Are you all right, Captain?

295
00:25:16,975 --> 00:25:18,143
Oh, yes.

296
00:25:18,226 --> 00:25:20,645
It's just to die for something

297
00:25:20,729 --> 00:25:22,981
as petty as slipping on tea.

298
00:25:23,064 --> 00:25:25,483
You're blaming yourself, Captain.
It was an accident.

299
00:25:25,567 --> 00:25:27,986
You'll feel better
when we put in.

300
00:25:28,069 --> 00:25:29,112
Put in?

301
00:25:29,195 --> 00:25:30,822
Well, to base, Captain.

302
00:25:30,905 --> 00:25:33,324
We cannot go on without
a political officer.

303
00:25:33,408 --> 00:25:36,870
Doctor, this is a combat vessel
of the Soviet Navy,

304
00:25:36,911 --> 00:25:39,330
and I'm a senior combat officer.

305
00:25:39,414 --> 00:25:42,792
We do not cancel operations
because of accidents.

306
00:25:44,377 --> 00:25:45,628
Seaman.

307
00:25:45,712 --> 00:25:47,964
Come here.

308
00:25:48,048 --> 00:25:50,550
Come here.

309
00:25:50,633 --> 00:25:52,260
Your name?

310
00:25:52,344 --> 00:25:54,054
Cook's Assistant, Loginov, sir.

311
00:25:54,137 --> 00:25:58,808
Good. I want you and the doctor
to witness this.

312
00:25:58,892 --> 00:26:00,977
I'm removing
the political officer's...

313
00:26:01,061 --> 00:26:02,354
missile key...

314
00:26:02,395 --> 00:26:03,688
carry on...

315
00:26:03,730 --> 00:26:05,815
and I'm keeping it myself.

316
00:26:05,899 --> 00:26:07,859
Captain, I think
we should report this

317
00:26:07,901 --> 00:26:09,778
to Red Fleet Command.

318
00:26:09,861 --> 00:26:11,071
I'm afraid
that's impossible, Doctor.

319
00:26:11,154 --> 00:26:13,573
Our orders are
for strict radio silence.

320
00:26:13,656 --> 00:26:15,158
Orders, sir.

321
00:26:15,241 --> 00:26:16,659
That's all, Loginov.

322
00:26:16,743 --> 00:26:17,660
Yes, sir.

323
00:26:17,744 --> 00:26:20,955
This is most unnerving,
Captain.

324
00:26:21,039 --> 00:26:24,501
The reason for having
two missile keys

325
00:26:24,584 --> 00:26:27,712
is so that no one man...

326
00:26:27,796 --> 00:26:28,797
may...

327
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:31,132
May what?

328
00:26:32,384 --> 00:26:34,052
May arm the missiles.

329
00:26:34,135 --> 00:26:36,054
Perhaps I should keep the key.

330
00:26:36,137 --> 00:26:38,098
Thank you.
That'll be all, Doctor.

331
00:26:38,181 --> 00:26:39,099
Captain, l...

332
00:26:39,182 --> 00:26:40,892
When I address the crew,

333
00:26:40,934 --> 00:26:43,395
then you will understand.

334
00:26:43,436 --> 00:26:44,688
Yes, sir.

335
00:26:47,273 --> 00:26:48,483
Petrov.

336
00:26:48,566 --> 00:26:49,651
Yes, sir?

337
00:26:49,734 --> 00:26:52,195
I will try to forget
your comments

338
00:26:52,278 --> 00:26:54,781
when I present my report.

339
00:26:54,864 --> 00:26:56,449
Thank you, sir.

340
00:27:01,871 --> 00:27:06,042
Holding steady on 3-1-0.

341
00:27:06,126 --> 00:27:08,044
12 knots
at about 20,000 yards.

342
00:27:08,128 --> 00:27:10,505
Have you got a make on him?

343
00:27:10,588 --> 00:27:12,048
Computer's chewing on it, sir.

344
00:27:12,132 --> 00:27:13,174
Twin screws.

345
00:27:13,258 --> 00:27:15,927
The plant noise
sounds like a typhoon.

346
00:27:24,978 --> 00:27:27,647
I'd say we got
a new boat, sir.

347
00:27:27,731 --> 00:27:30,525
Tommy, I miss something
on the boards?

348
00:27:30,608 --> 00:27:32,569
Sub plant hasn't said
anything about it.

349
00:27:32,652 --> 00:27:33,695
Hmm.

350
00:27:35,655 --> 00:27:36,698
All right.

351
00:27:36,781 --> 00:27:39,159
How many typhoons
we got in the computer?

352
00:27:39,242 --> 00:27:40,160
Six, sir.

353
00:27:40,243 --> 00:27:42,203
OK. Call this guy
Typhoon Seven.

354
00:27:42,287 --> 00:27:43,663
Start a tape on him.

355
00:27:43,747 --> 00:27:46,166
See if we can work
in a little closer.

356
00:27:46,249 --> 00:27:48,084
Yes, sir.

357
00:27:48,168 --> 00:27:49,586
Won't he hear us?

358
00:27:49,669 --> 00:27:52,630
Not if we stay in his baffles,
Seaman Beaumont.

359
00:27:52,714 --> 00:27:55,175
Not if we stay in his baffles.

360
00:27:55,258 --> 00:27:56,760
Come in behind his propeller,

361
00:27:56,843 --> 00:27:59,304
and he's deaf as a post.

362
00:28:27,832 --> 00:28:30,502
Any sonar contacts,
Mr. Kamarov?

363
00:28:30,585 --> 00:28:33,213
No contacts, Captain.
The sonar is clear.

364
00:28:33,296 --> 00:28:36,174
Good. Do we have any
surface contacts, Mr. Borodin?

365
00:28:36,216 --> 00:28:38,677
No contacts, sir.
Scope is clear.

366
00:28:38,718 --> 00:28:40,720
Good. Then it is time

367
00:28:40,762 --> 00:28:43,390
I explain our orders
to the crew.

368
00:28:44,683 --> 00:28:47,227
Comrades, this is your captain.

369
00:28:47,310 --> 00:28:50,188
It is an honor
to speak to you today,

370
00:28:50,271 --> 00:28:52,732
and I'm honored
to be sailing with you

371
00:28:52,816 --> 00:28:54,192
on the maiden voyage

372
00:28:54,275 --> 00:28:56,277
of our motherland's
most recent achievement.

373
00:28:56,361 --> 00:29:01,366
And once more,
we play our dangerous game,

374
00:29:01,449 --> 00:29:03,118
a game of chess,

375
00:29:03,201 --> 00:29:05,662
against our old adversary...

376
00:29:05,745 --> 00:29:07,414
the American Navy.

377
00:29:07,497 --> 00:29:08,915
For 40 years,

378
00:29:08,998 --> 00:29:12,168
your fathers before you
and your older brothers

379
00:29:12,252 --> 00:29:14,838
played this game
and played it well.

380
00:29:14,921 --> 00:29:17,716
But today,
the game is different.

381
00:29:17,799 --> 00:29:20,343
We have the advantage.

382
00:29:20,427 --> 00:29:22,929
It reminds me of the heady days

383
00:29:23,013 --> 00:29:24,431
of Sputnik and Yuri Gagarin,

384
00:29:24,514 --> 00:29:27,934
when the world trembled
at the sound of our rockets.

385
00:29:28,018 --> 00:29:29,936
Well, they will tremble again

386
00:29:30,020 --> 00:29:31,938
at the sound of our silence.

387
00:29:32,022 --> 00:29:34,941
The order is engage
for silent drive.

388
00:29:35,025 --> 00:29:36,151
Aye, sir.

389
00:29:36,234 --> 00:29:38,486
Ballast control,
open outer doors.

390
00:29:38,570 --> 00:29:39,988
Yes, open outer doors.

391
00:29:40,071 --> 00:29:41,114
Diving command.

392
00:29:41,197 --> 00:29:43,658
Engage caterpillar
and secure main engines.

393
00:29:48,288 --> 00:29:49,956
Doors opening, Captain.

394
00:29:50,040 --> 00:29:51,458
Comrades, our own fleet

395
00:29:51,541 --> 00:29:54,502
doesn't know
our full potential.

396
00:29:54,586 --> 00:29:57,255
They will do everything
possible to test us,

397
00:29:57,339 --> 00:30:00,258
but they will only test
their own embarrassment.

398
00:30:00,342 --> 00:30:02,761
We will leave our fleet behind.

399
00:30:02,844 --> 00:30:05,305
We will pass through
the American patrols,

400
00:30:05,388 --> 00:30:07,057
pass their sonar nets,

401
00:30:07,140 --> 00:30:09,934
and lay off their largest city

402
00:30:10,018 --> 00:30:12,854
and listen
to their rock 'n' roll

403
00:30:12,937 --> 00:30:15,982
while we conduct missile drills.

404
00:30:16,066 --> 00:30:18,193
Then...

405
00:30:18,276 --> 00:30:19,611
And when we are finished,

406
00:30:19,652 --> 00:30:21,571
the only sound they will hear

407
00:30:21,654 --> 00:30:23,239
is our laughter

408
00:30:23,323 --> 00:30:25,200
while we sail to Havana,

409
00:30:25,283 --> 00:30:28,828
where the sun is warm
and so is the comradeship.

410
00:30:31,039 --> 00:30:33,083
Cryogenic plant
coming on line, Captain.

411
00:30:33,166 --> 00:30:35,710
A great day, comrades.

412
00:30:35,794 --> 00:30:37,712
We sail into history.

413
00:30:44,344 --> 00:30:46,012
Caterpillar engaging.

414
00:30:49,015 --> 00:30:51,559
Caterpillar engaging,
Captain.

415
00:31:05,907 --> 00:31:07,075
What happened?

416
00:31:07,158 --> 00:31:08,618
I don't know.

417
00:31:35,186 --> 00:31:36,604
Full rudder left.

418
00:31:36,688 --> 00:31:38,148
Full rudder left.

419
00:31:38,231 --> 00:31:39,149
Navigator.

420
00:31:39,232 --> 00:31:41,943
New course, 2-5-0.

421
00:31:42,027 --> 00:31:43,987
Yes, new course, 2-5-0.

422
00:31:44,070 --> 00:31:45,363
Steering 2-5-0.

423
00:31:45,405 --> 00:31:47,198
Yes, course is 2-5-0.

424
00:32:19,022 --> 00:32:20,482
Sonar contact, Captain.
Port quarter.

425
00:32:20,565 --> 00:32:21,733
Close aboard.

426
00:32:21,816 --> 00:32:23,068
American,

427
00:32:23,151 --> 00:32:24,736
Los Angeles class.

428
00:32:25,737 --> 00:32:27,238
American. Captain, we...

429
00:32:27,322 --> 00:32:29,908
Course 3-1-0.

430
00:32:29,991 --> 00:32:33,328
Is the American
turning to follow us?

431
00:32:33,411 --> 00:32:34,746
No, Captain.

432
00:32:34,829 --> 00:32:37,957
The American is continuing
in his original course.

433
00:32:38,041 --> 00:32:39,751
He's continuing northwest.

434
00:32:39,834 --> 00:32:41,795
He can't hear us.

435
00:32:41,878 --> 00:32:43,922
And the singing?

436
00:32:57,894 --> 00:32:59,604
Let them sing.

437
00:33:19,332 --> 00:33:21,418
What are you talking about?
Check your gear.

438
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:23,086
Running diagnostics now,
Captain.

439
00:33:29,300 --> 00:33:31,678
Sonar is working, Captain.

440
00:33:31,761 --> 00:33:33,263
The Russian disappeared.

441
00:33:33,346 --> 00:33:34,806
One minute, he was steady

442
00:33:34,848 --> 00:33:36,307
4,000 yards off the bow,

443
00:33:36,349 --> 00:33:37,892
and then he was gone,

444
00:33:37,976 --> 00:33:41,813
and for a second,
I thought I heard...

445
00:33:41,855 --> 00:33:44,149
Heard what?

446
00:33:44,190 --> 00:33:46,735
I thought I heard singing, sir.

447
00:33:48,278 --> 00:33:49,487
Singing?

448
00:33:49,571 --> 00:33:50,989
Yes, sir.

449
00:34:05,253 --> 00:34:06,379
Yah, yah.

450
00:34:09,132 --> 00:34:10,842
Yah, yah, yah.

451
00:34:13,928 --> 00:34:15,347
Yah, yah.

452
00:34:18,808 --> 00:34:20,185
Good morning, sir.

453
00:34:20,268 --> 00:34:22,687
I hope we had a lovely evening.

454
00:34:22,771 --> 00:34:23,855
Yah, yah.

455
00:34:23,938 --> 00:34:26,024
A little chilly this morning.

456
00:34:49,756 --> 00:34:52,801
There's a letter there
from Marko Ramius.

457
00:34:55,095 --> 00:34:56,971
Ah, Marko.

458
00:35:28,878 --> 00:35:30,255
Yes?

459
00:35:30,338 --> 00:35:32,424
No. This is Dr Ryan.

460
00:35:36,761 --> 00:35:38,221
What the hell happened?

461
00:35:38,304 --> 00:35:39,347
Come on.

462
00:35:39,431 --> 00:35:42,350
This is no longer
a research project.

463
00:35:45,061 --> 00:35:47,981
I got a line on those doors.

464
00:35:49,607 --> 00:35:51,526
You know what they are?

465
00:35:53,737 --> 00:35:56,614
A nearly silent
propulsion system?

466
00:35:56,656 --> 00:35:58,616
How did you know that?

467
00:35:58,658 --> 00:36:01,619
The captain of the sub
following her radioed in.

468
00:36:01,661 --> 00:36:04,122
Thing disappeared
right in front of him,

469
00:36:04,164 --> 00:36:06,624
and that's only
the half of it.

470
00:36:06,666 --> 00:36:07,792
Read.

471
00:36:14,466 --> 00:36:16,009
Oh! I'm sorry.

472
00:36:17,552 --> 00:36:18,762
Got your I.D.?

473
00:36:18,845 --> 00:36:20,930
My God, this is unbelievable.

474
00:36:21,014 --> 00:36:23,641
The Kirov, three Sverdlovs,

475
00:36:23,725 --> 00:36:25,352
the Kiev, the Minsk.

476
00:36:25,435 --> 00:36:27,354
They've sortied
their whole bloody fleet.

477
00:36:27,437 --> 00:36:29,356
That's about the size of it.

478
00:36:29,439 --> 00:36:30,857
Good afternoon, Admiral.

479
00:36:30,940 --> 00:36:31,816
Afternoon, Matthew.

480
00:36:31,858 --> 00:36:33,943
Where are we going anyway?

481
00:36:34,027 --> 00:36:36,112
Briefing for Jeffrey Pelt,

482
00:36:36,154 --> 00:36:38,531
President's National
Security Adviser.

483
00:36:38,615 --> 00:36:41,326
Most of the Joint Chiefs
will be there,

484
00:36:41,409 --> 00:36:43,661
along with a few other people.

485
00:36:43,745 --> 00:36:45,413
Who's giving the briefing?

486
00:36:45,497 --> 00:36:46,539
You are.

487
00:36:46,623 --> 00:36:49,209
The yeoman will
have all the slides

488
00:36:49,292 --> 00:36:50,710
Iaid out for you.

489
00:36:50,794 --> 00:36:52,545
So all you got to do...

490
00:36:57,467 --> 00:36:59,219
Look.

491
00:36:59,302 --> 00:37:02,222
No one understands
this material better than you.

492
00:37:02,305 --> 00:37:04,724
Just give him
a rundown on the sub

493
00:37:04,808 --> 00:37:07,686
and a precis of the
stuff in your hand.

494
00:37:07,769 --> 00:37:10,313
He's liable to ask
some direct questions.

495
00:37:10,355 --> 00:37:11,731
Give him direct answers.

496
00:37:11,815 --> 00:37:13,483
Tell him what you think.

497
00:37:14,693 --> 00:37:15,902
Come on.

498
00:37:17,654 --> 00:37:19,406
Ahem.

499
00:37:19,489 --> 00:37:20,573
Ahem.

500
00:37:21,825 --> 00:37:24,285
Uh, gentlemen,
the last 24 hours

501
00:37:24,369 --> 00:37:27,997
have seen some extraordinary
Soviet naval activity.

502
00:37:28,081 --> 00:37:31,042
The first to sail
was this ship,

503
00:37:31,084 --> 00:37:33,044
we believe
called the Red October

504
00:37:33,086 --> 00:37:35,672
in reference to
the October Revolution of 1917.

505
00:37:35,755 --> 00:37:38,216
A variant
of the typhoon class,

506
00:37:38,299 --> 00:37:40,218
she's some 650 feet long

507
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:42,762
and 32,000 tons
submerged displacement...

508
00:37:42,846 --> 00:37:44,264
roughly the same size

509
00:37:44,347 --> 00:37:47,308
as a World War Il
aircraft carrier.

510
00:37:47,392 --> 00:37:49,269
We believe that these doors,

511
00:37:49,352 --> 00:37:52,272
here on the bow
and again on the stern,

512
00:37:52,355 --> 00:37:54,315
enclose a unique
propulsion system...

513
00:37:54,399 --> 00:37:57,444
a magneto-hydrodynamic drive,
or caterpillar,

514
00:37:57,527 --> 00:38:00,989
that would enable the sub
to run virtually silent.

515
00:38:01,072 --> 00:38:03,616
It is possible
that this new drive system

516
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:06,161
allowed the captain,
a man named Ramius,

517
00:38:06,244 --> 00:38:08,663
their senior and perhaps
most respected commander...

518
00:38:08,747 --> 00:38:10,707
Do we have
his picture available?

519
00:38:10,749 --> 00:38:13,585
Allowed Ramius to elude
one of our attack boats,

520
00:38:13,668 --> 00:38:17,088
the Dallas, which trailed Red October
from harbor this morning.

521
00:38:18,882 --> 00:38:20,300
It is also possible

522
00:38:20,383 --> 00:38:22,802
that this drive system,
if operable,

523
00:38:22,886 --> 00:38:24,804
could render the Red October

524
00:38:24,888 --> 00:38:27,349
undetectable to
our Sosus warning nets

525
00:38:27,432 --> 00:38:28,808
in the Atlantic.

526
00:38:28,892 --> 00:38:31,311
Mr. Ryan, would you
characterize this

527
00:38:31,394 --> 00:38:33,480
as a first-strike weapon?

528
00:38:36,399 --> 00:38:38,860
Uh, that is a possibility, sir.

529
00:38:38,943 --> 00:38:41,363
It is designed
to approach by stealth

530
00:38:41,446 --> 00:38:42,822
and to shower its target

531
00:38:42,906 --> 00:38:44,324
with multiple
independent warheads

532
00:38:44,407 --> 00:38:46,534
with little or no warning
before impact.

533
00:38:46,618 --> 00:38:50,080
Goddamn thing's made
to start a war.

534
00:38:51,081 --> 00:38:52,540
Proceed, Mr. Ryan.

535
00:38:52,624 --> 00:38:55,502
About the same time
the Dallas lost contact,

536
00:38:55,585 --> 00:38:57,045
there were additional sailings

537
00:38:57,128 --> 00:39:00,131
from Polijarny and from
Leningrad on the Baltic

538
00:39:00,215 --> 00:39:01,966
and from the Mediterranean.

539
00:39:02,050 --> 00:39:04,719
There are now
some 58 nuclear submarines

540
00:39:04,803 --> 00:39:07,347
headed at high speed
into the Atlantic.

541
00:39:07,430 --> 00:39:10,100
This afternoon a satellite pass
over Polijarny

542
00:39:10,141 --> 00:39:12,686
found heat blooms
in the engineering plants

543
00:39:12,769 --> 00:39:14,396
at the Kirov, the Minsk,

544
00:39:14,479 --> 00:39:16,898
and more than 20 other
cruisers and destroyers,

545
00:39:16,981 --> 00:39:19,192
indicating they were
preparing to sail.

546
00:39:19,275 --> 00:39:22,445
This constitutes the bulk
of the Soviet surface fleet.

547
00:39:22,487 --> 00:39:23,947
Admiral Greer,
your conclusions.

548
00:39:23,988 --> 00:39:25,323
Well, sir,

549
00:39:25,365 --> 00:39:28,284
the data support
no conclusions as yet.

550
00:39:28,326 --> 00:39:31,162
The absence of activity
in the Pacific

551
00:39:31,246 --> 00:39:33,665
suggests this could
be just an exercise.

552
00:39:33,748 --> 00:39:35,709
It may have nothing to do...

553
00:39:35,792 --> 00:39:37,711
Suppose it's not an exercise?

554
00:39:37,794 --> 00:39:39,170
Suppose this
is the beginning

555
00:39:39,254 --> 00:39:40,672
of a move against NATO?

556
00:39:40,755 --> 00:39:42,674
NSA can speak for that,
Mr. Pelt.

557
00:39:42,757 --> 00:39:45,885
I must emphasize the extreme
sensitivity of this information

558
00:39:45,969 --> 00:39:48,388
and that it not leave the room.

559
00:39:48,471 --> 00:39:49,889
Before sailing, Captain Ramius

560
00:39:49,973 --> 00:39:52,559
sent a letter
to Admiral Yuri Padorin,

561
00:39:52,642 --> 00:39:54,102
Chairman of the Red Fleet

562
00:39:54,185 --> 00:39:55,895
northern political directorate.

563
00:39:55,979 --> 00:39:57,439
That's her uncle.

564
00:39:57,522 --> 00:39:59,232
Whose uncle?

565
00:39:59,315 --> 00:40:00,775
Ramius' wife.

566
00:40:00,859 --> 00:40:02,902
Padorin's her uncle.

567
00:40:02,986 --> 00:40:06,740
Now, the contents
of the letter are unknown,

568
00:40:06,823 --> 00:40:09,576
but Admiral Padorin
immediately demanded

569
00:40:09,659 --> 00:40:11,786
a meeting
with Premier Chernenko,

570
00:40:11,870 --> 00:40:14,247
and within minutes
of that meeting,

571
00:40:14,330 --> 00:40:15,707
the Soviet fleet sailed

572
00:40:15,790 --> 00:40:18,251
with orders
to find Red October

573
00:40:18,335 --> 00:40:19,919
and sink her.

574
00:40:21,171 --> 00:40:22,255
Sink her?

575
00:40:22,339 --> 00:40:24,758
My God. They've got
a madman on their hands.

576
00:40:24,841 --> 00:40:27,594
If it gets within
500 miles of the coast,

577
00:40:27,677 --> 00:40:30,597
we'll have less than
two minutes' warning.

578
00:40:45,612 --> 00:40:47,322
Today's the 23rd, isn't it?

579
00:40:47,405 --> 00:40:48,365
What?

580
00:40:48,406 --> 00:40:49,491
Is today the 23rd?

581
00:40:49,574 --> 00:40:50,492
Yeah.

582
00:41:01,252 --> 00:41:03,546
You son of a bitch.

583
00:41:04,714 --> 00:41:07,634
You son of a bitch!

584
00:41:10,553 --> 00:41:12,389
You wish to add something

585
00:41:12,430 --> 00:41:14,099
to our discussion, Dr Ryan?

586
00:41:19,562 --> 00:41:21,648
Well, sir,
I was just thinking

587
00:41:21,731 --> 00:41:24,818
that perhaps there's another
possibility we might consider.

588
00:41:26,444 --> 00:41:28,697
Ramius might be
trying to defect.

589
00:41:28,780 --> 00:41:29,906
Do you mean to suggest

590
00:41:29,948 --> 00:41:31,449
that this man has come...

591
00:41:31,491 --> 00:41:33,284
Proceed, Mr. Ryan.

592
00:41:36,287 --> 00:41:37,539
Well...

593
00:41:37,622 --> 00:41:40,208
Ramius trained most
of their officer corps,

594
00:41:40,291 --> 00:41:42,711
which would put him
in a position

595
00:41:42,794 --> 00:41:45,714
to select men
willing to help him.

596
00:41:45,797 --> 00:41:47,382
And he's not Russian.

597
00:41:48,383 --> 00:41:49,884
He's Lithuanian by birth,

598
00:41:49,968 --> 00:41:52,887
raised by his paternal
grandfather, a fisherman.

599
00:41:52,971 --> 00:41:57,058
And he has no children,
no ties to leave behind.

600
00:41:59,811 --> 00:42:02,522
And today is
the first anniversary

601
00:42:02,605 --> 00:42:03,982
of his wife's death.

602
00:42:04,065 --> 00:42:05,984
Come on.
You're just an analyst.

603
00:42:06,067 --> 00:42:08,278
How can you read his mind?

604
00:42:08,319 --> 00:42:09,946
I know Ramius, General.

605
00:42:09,988 --> 00:42:12,991
He's nearly a legend
in the submarine community.

606
00:42:13,033 --> 00:42:15,285
He's been a maverick
his entire career.

607
00:42:15,326 --> 00:42:18,121
I actually met him once
at an embassy dinner.

608
00:42:18,163 --> 00:42:20,749
Have you ever met
Captain Ramius, General?

609
00:42:29,549 --> 00:42:30,967
Admiral Hollis,

610
00:42:31,009 --> 00:42:34,471
how long before Ramius
could be in a position

611
00:42:34,554 --> 00:42:37,015
to fire his missiles at us?

612
00:42:37,057 --> 00:42:38,308
Four days.

613
00:42:38,350 --> 00:42:40,810
All right.
I'll brief the President.

614
00:42:40,852 --> 00:42:43,063
That will be all, gentlemen.

615
00:42:43,146 --> 00:42:44,481
Dr Ryan,

616
00:42:44,522 --> 00:42:48,151
would you stay
for a moment, please?

617
00:42:48,193 --> 00:42:51,321
I said speak your mind,
Jack, but Jesus...

618
00:43:03,249 --> 00:43:07,045
You slammed the door
on the general pretty hard, Jack.

619
00:43:07,087 --> 00:43:09,547
That was not my intention, sir.

620
00:43:09,589 --> 00:43:11,049
Oh, yes, it was.

621
00:43:11,091 --> 00:43:12,509
He was patronizing you,

622
00:43:12,550 --> 00:43:14,511
and you stomped on him.

623
00:43:14,552 --> 00:43:17,013
In my opinion,
he deserved it.

624
00:43:17,055 --> 00:43:19,182
Listen, I'm a politician,

625
00:43:19,224 --> 00:43:21,685
which means I'm
a cheat and a liar,

626
00:43:21,726 --> 00:43:23,853
and when I'm
not kissing babies,

627
00:43:23,937 --> 00:43:25,522
I'm stealing
their lollipops,

628
00:43:25,563 --> 00:43:30,777
but it also means
that I keep my options open.

629
00:43:30,860 --> 00:43:37,033
So let's assume for a minute
that you're right

630
00:43:37,117 --> 00:43:39,703
and this Russian
intends to defect.

631
00:43:40,745 --> 00:43:43,373
What do you suggest
we do about it?

632
00:43:45,250 --> 00:43:47,168
We definitely
grab the boat, sir.

633
00:43:47,252 --> 00:43:49,337
Hey, wait a minute.

634
00:43:49,421 --> 00:43:52,757
We're not talking about
some stray pilot with a M.I.G.

635
00:43:52,841 --> 00:43:55,260
We're talking about several
billion dollars' worth

636
00:43:55,343 --> 00:43:56,761
of Soviet state property.

637
00:43:56,845 --> 00:43:58,763
They're going to want it back.

638
00:43:58,847 --> 00:44:00,265
Maybe it's enough then

639
00:44:00,306 --> 00:44:03,268
just to get some people
onboard and inspect it.

640
00:44:03,351 --> 00:44:05,270
Call it whatever
you want to...

641
00:44:05,353 --> 00:44:07,313
a Coast Guard
safety inspection.

642
00:44:07,397 --> 00:44:10,108
So how do we proceed?

643
00:44:10,191 --> 00:44:11,609
First, we need to contact

644
00:44:11,651 --> 00:44:14,612
the commanders
in the Atlantic directly.

645
00:44:14,654 --> 00:44:16,614
The Russians get
one whiff of this

646
00:44:16,656 --> 00:44:18,283
through the regular
communications circuits,

647
00:44:18,366 --> 00:44:19,784
the game is up.

648
00:44:19,868 --> 00:44:22,787
Second, we figure out
how we can help them.

649
00:44:22,871 --> 00:44:24,789
We devise a plan to intercede,

650
00:44:24,873 --> 00:44:27,292
ready to go at a moment's notice.

651
00:44:27,375 --> 00:44:30,378
Third, somebody's got to
go make contact with Ramius

652
00:44:30,462 --> 00:44:33,214
and find out what
his intentions really are.

653
00:44:33,298 --> 00:44:34,507
OK.

654
00:44:34,591 --> 00:44:35,967
When do you leave?

655
00:44:36,051 --> 00:44:37,510
Ha ha ha!

656
00:44:37,594 --> 00:44:39,637
Wait a minute.

657
00:44:40,930 --> 00:44:42,349
The general was right.

658
00:44:42,432 --> 00:44:43,808
I am not field personnel.

659
00:44:43,892 --> 00:44:45,310
I am only an analyst.

660
00:44:45,393 --> 00:44:46,478
You're perfect.

661
00:44:46,561 --> 00:44:49,356
I can't ask any
of these characters to go.

662
00:44:49,439 --> 00:44:50,857
They don't believe in it.

663
00:44:50,940 --> 00:44:52,484
They'd never stake
their reputation

664
00:44:52,567 --> 00:44:53,985
on a hunch, whereas you...

665
00:44:54,069 --> 00:44:55,487
Are expendable.

666
00:44:57,947 --> 00:44:59,491
Something like that.

667
00:45:02,911 --> 00:45:04,871
I'll give you three days

668
00:45:04,954 --> 00:45:06,831
to prove your theory correct.

669
00:45:06,915 --> 00:45:08,333
After that,

670
00:45:08,416 --> 00:45:11,836
I have to hunt down Ramius
and destroy him.

671
00:45:11,920 --> 00:45:13,838
Will you do it?

672
00:45:30,313 --> 00:45:32,273
Mother of God!

673
00:45:32,357 --> 00:45:33,900
Secure the mast!

674
00:45:33,983 --> 00:45:35,402
All ahead, 2/3!

675
00:45:35,485 --> 00:45:36,903
Set depth to 900 meters!

676
00:45:36,986 --> 00:45:38,905
What is it?
What's happened?

677
00:45:38,988 --> 00:45:41,032
Mast secured, helm at 2/3.

678
00:45:42,659 --> 00:45:45,036
Commence heading 2-0-5.

679
00:45:45,120 --> 00:45:47,539
2-0-5, affirmative.

680
00:46:00,885 --> 00:46:03,722
These orders
are seven bloody hours old!

681
00:46:03,805 --> 00:46:05,265
Sitting on the bottom

682
00:46:05,348 --> 00:46:06,725
Iike an addled schoolboy.

683
00:46:06,808 --> 00:46:07,767
Captain...

684
00:46:07,851 --> 00:46:08,768
Passing at...

685
00:46:08,852 --> 00:46:09,894
All ahead, flank.

686
00:46:09,978 --> 00:46:11,062
All ahead, flank.

687
00:46:11,146 --> 00:46:13,231
Inquire of the engineer
about the possibility

688
00:46:16,985 --> 00:46:18,903
Seven hours.

689
00:46:18,987 --> 00:46:21,573
The entire fleet is after him.

690
00:46:30,623 --> 00:46:32,125
Leveling at 900 meters.

691
00:46:32,208 --> 00:46:33,293
Very well.

692
00:46:33,376 --> 00:46:35,253
Captain, Engineer reports

693
00:46:37,881 --> 00:46:39,424
but not recommended.

694
00:46:50,352 --> 00:46:52,979
Go to 105 on the reactor.

695
00:46:55,899 --> 00:46:57,275
Captain, what is it?

696
00:46:57,359 --> 00:46:58,777
Where are we going?

697
00:46:58,860 --> 00:47:01,446
We're going to
kill a friend, Yevgeni.

698
00:47:02,322 --> 00:47:04,991
We're going to kill Ramius.

699
00:47:26,680 --> 00:47:28,139
If you like borscht,
perhaps,

700
00:47:28,181 --> 00:47:30,600
but I've eaten better
in an oiler's galley.

701
00:47:30,642 --> 00:47:32,519
My wife said to the waiter,

702
00:47:32,602 --> 00:47:35,063
"Where did this man
learn to cook,

703
00:47:35,146 --> 00:47:36,106
Afghanistan?"

704
00:47:36,147 --> 00:47:38,942
So then we went
on to the Bolshoi,

705
00:47:38,983 --> 00:47:40,193
the ballet,

706
00:47:40,276 --> 00:47:42,445
to see this new girl
as Giselle.

707
00:47:42,529 --> 00:47:44,114
You remember
how beautiful she was.

708
00:47:44,155 --> 00:47:46,116
She just married
a factory manager.

709
00:47:46,157 --> 00:47:47,575
Excuse me, Doctor.

710
00:47:47,659 --> 00:47:49,577
Do you have the figures

711
00:47:49,661 --> 00:47:52,622
for the latest batch
of radiation tests?

712
00:47:52,664 --> 00:47:53,748
Now?

713
00:47:54,666 --> 00:47:56,084
Now.

714
00:47:57,669 --> 00:48:01,256
Perhaps you could bring
the preceding set as well...

715
00:48:02,173 --> 00:48:03,591
for comparison.

716
00:48:04,676 --> 00:48:06,261
Thank you, Doctor.

717
00:48:24,195 --> 00:48:26,781
It will take him some time.

718
00:48:27,866 --> 00:48:30,493
Before we begin, Captain,

719
00:48:30,535 --> 00:48:31,953
I'd like to know

720
00:48:32,037 --> 00:48:33,872
exactly what happened
to Putin.

721
00:48:39,044 --> 00:48:42,005
He didn't slip
on his tea, did he?

722
00:48:43,214 --> 00:48:45,633
I don't think I like
your tone, Slavin.

723
00:48:45,717 --> 00:48:49,137
What the hell's my tone
got to do with it?

724
00:48:49,220 --> 00:48:50,638
We're risking our lives here.

725
00:48:50,722 --> 00:48:53,141
Putin could've caused
complications.

726
00:48:53,224 --> 00:48:55,685
Did you think he would
just go away and sulk

727
00:48:55,727 --> 00:48:57,687
while we carried out our plans?

728
00:48:57,729 --> 00:48:59,981
Are you saying
he was murdered?

729
00:49:00,065 --> 00:49:01,649
My God.

730
00:49:01,733 --> 00:49:03,151
Stop whining, Yuri.

731
00:49:03,234 --> 00:49:04,652
How can you justify murder?

732
00:49:04,736 --> 00:49:06,196
So he was murdered.

733
00:49:06,237 --> 00:49:08,365
The man was a pig,

734
00:49:08,448 --> 00:49:11,242
but it's a decision
we should've all made together.

735
00:49:11,284 --> 00:49:12,744
You are not in command here.

736
00:49:12,786 --> 00:49:15,663
If the crew finds out,
we could have a mutiny.

737
00:49:15,747 --> 00:49:17,248
What are you talking about?

738
00:49:17,332 --> 00:49:18,750
They could kill us all.

739
00:49:18,792 --> 00:49:21,753
Everything that happens
on this ship affects us all.

740
00:49:21,836 --> 00:49:24,255
The Master-At-Arms
is already suspicious.

741
00:49:24,297 --> 00:49:25,757
We could still go back.

742
00:49:25,799 --> 00:49:28,051
There will be no going back.

743
00:49:30,136 --> 00:49:31,554
Before we sailed,

744
00:49:31,596 --> 00:49:35,892
I dispatched a letter
to Admiral Padorin,

745
00:49:35,934 --> 00:49:38,520
in which I announced
our intention...

746
00:49:38,603 --> 00:49:40,146
to defect.

747
00:49:41,731 --> 00:49:44,651
In the name of God, why?

748
00:49:44,734 --> 00:49:47,153
When he reached the New World,

749
00:49:47,237 --> 00:49:49,656
Cortez burned his ships.

750
00:49:49,739 --> 00:49:53,284
As a result, his men
were well motivated.

751
00:49:53,368 --> 00:49:56,162
You have signed
our death warrants.

752
00:49:56,246 --> 00:49:59,207
Padorin will send
the entire fleet.

753
00:49:59,290 --> 00:50:00,667
Jesus, they'll find us.

754
00:50:00,750 --> 00:50:02,043
They'll find us and...

755
00:50:02,127 --> 00:50:03,878
No one's going to find us.

756
00:50:03,962 --> 00:50:05,505
That's enough, Yuri.

757
00:50:06,756 --> 00:50:08,425
You had to do it, huh?

758
00:50:08,466 --> 00:50:11,428
You couldn't just
turn the submarine over

759
00:50:11,469 --> 00:50:12,929
to the Americans.

760
00:50:12,971 --> 00:50:15,932
You had to make
a political statement.

761
00:50:15,974 --> 00:50:19,519
Or was it something
deeper, Captain...

762
00:50:19,602 --> 00:50:23,398
something that made you
unable to slip away?

763
00:50:23,481 --> 00:50:26,234
Was it ego, Captain?

764
00:50:26,317 --> 00:50:29,237
We each have
our reasons, Victor.

765
00:50:29,320 --> 00:50:30,697
My own began

766
00:50:30,780 --> 00:50:34,743
the day I was handed
the blueprints for this ship...

767
00:50:34,826 --> 00:50:37,746
a ship which had but one use.

768
00:50:37,829 --> 00:50:39,706
And as for the rest,

769
00:50:39,789 --> 00:50:42,709
well, those are things
that I alone

770
00:50:42,792 --> 00:50:44,711
must carry the burden for.

771
00:50:44,794 --> 00:50:49,257
Anatoli,
you're afraid of our fleet.

772
00:50:49,341 --> 00:50:51,176
Well, you should be.

773
00:50:51,259 --> 00:50:55,555
Personally, I give us
one chance in three.

774
00:50:59,309 --> 00:51:01,436
More tea, anyone?

775
00:51:04,439 --> 00:51:06,149
No?

776
00:51:07,317 --> 00:51:10,570
Then you may report
back to your posts.

777
00:51:27,379 --> 00:51:30,090
You heard the Captain.
Dismissed.

778
00:51:39,391 --> 00:51:41,059
Captain.

779
00:51:52,862 --> 00:51:55,323
Captain, I would never
disagree with you

780
00:51:55,407 --> 00:51:58,284
in front of the men,
you know that.

781
00:51:58,368 --> 00:52:01,788
But in this case,
Victor is right.

782
00:52:02,706 --> 00:52:04,165
It would've been better

783
00:52:04,249 --> 00:52:06,626
if you had not informed Moscow.

784
00:52:06,710 --> 00:52:07,877
Oh, Vasily.

785
00:52:07,919 --> 00:52:10,547
Moscow is not the worry,

786
00:52:10,588 --> 00:52:13,174
nor the whole Soviet Navy.

787
00:52:13,258 --> 00:52:15,677
I know their tactics.

788
00:52:15,760 --> 00:52:18,013
I have the advantage.

789
00:52:19,764 --> 00:52:21,016
No.

790
00:52:21,933 --> 00:52:24,519
The worry is the Americans.

791
00:52:25,770 --> 00:52:28,565
If we meet the right sort,

792
00:52:28,606 --> 00:52:30,859
this will work.

793
00:52:33,278 --> 00:52:37,198
We get some buckaroo...

794
00:52:41,786 --> 00:52:44,247
Some turbulence,
eh, Commander?

795
00:52:44,289 --> 00:52:46,750
You don't like flying, huh?

796
00:52:46,791 --> 00:52:48,209
Oh, this is nothing!

797
00:52:48,293 --> 00:52:49,711
You should've been with us

798
00:52:49,794 --> 00:52:51,212
five, six months ago!

799
00:52:51,296 --> 00:52:52,839
Oh! You talk about puke!

800
00:52:52,922 --> 00:52:55,842
We ran into a hailstorm
over the sea of Japan, right?

801
00:52:55,925 --> 00:52:58,094
Everybody's retching
their guts out!

802
00:52:58,178 --> 00:53:00,221
The pilot shot his lunch

803
00:53:00,305 --> 00:53:01,222
all over the windshield,

804
00:53:01,306 --> 00:53:03,725
and I barf on the radio!

805
00:53:03,808 --> 00:53:04,934
Knocked it out completely,

806
00:53:04,976 --> 00:53:06,978
and it wasn't
that lightweight stuff either!

807
00:53:07,020 --> 00:53:09,522
It was that chunky,
industrial-weight puke!

808
00:53:09,606 --> 00:53:11,107
You want a bite?

809
00:53:11,149 --> 00:53:14,152
Jack, next time
you get a bright idea,

810
00:53:14,194 --> 00:53:17,072
just put it in a memo.

811
00:53:25,663 --> 00:53:26,664
Sir.

812
00:53:28,208 --> 00:53:30,794
That'll be all, Lieutenant.
Thank you.

813
00:53:30,835 --> 00:53:32,837
That hawkeye
from Weymouth Trap?

814
00:53:32,879 --> 00:53:33,797
Four wire.

815
00:53:33,838 --> 00:53:36,341
Caught a gust over the fantail.

816
00:53:36,383 --> 00:53:37,801
Well, not bad, considering.

817
00:53:37,842 --> 00:53:40,845
The gentleman
is here to see you.

818
00:53:40,887 --> 00:53:41,971
The gentleman?

819
00:53:42,013 --> 00:53:43,765
What the hell
you talking about, Charlie?

820
00:53:43,848 --> 00:53:45,725
I apologize
for the uniform, sir.

821
00:53:45,809 --> 00:53:47,394
This is Admiral Greer's idea

822
00:53:47,477 --> 00:53:48,853
of a low profile.

823
00:53:48,937 --> 00:53:50,855
You work for Jim Greer?

824
00:53:50,897 --> 00:53:52,357
That's right, sir.

825
00:53:52,440 --> 00:53:54,859
Then I imagine you'll tell me

826
00:53:54,901 --> 00:53:56,695
what all the hubbub's about.

827
00:53:57,821 --> 00:53:59,739
Captain, you got a minute?

828
00:53:59,823 --> 00:54:01,241
Jonesy's got something

829
00:54:01,324 --> 00:54:03,201
I think you ought to see.

830
00:54:03,284 --> 00:54:05,578
Martinez, get yourself
a cup of coffee.

831
00:54:05,662 --> 00:54:07,330
Send me up some tea.

832
00:54:07,414 --> 00:54:08,373
Aye, Captain.

833
00:54:08,456 --> 00:54:12,252
Messenger,
get the captain a cup of tea.

834
00:54:14,379 --> 00:54:15,922
Captain.

835
00:54:17,340 --> 00:54:20,260
Sir, the moment
that sub went silent

836
00:54:20,343 --> 00:54:22,721
and I thought I
heard some singing,

837
00:54:22,804 --> 00:54:25,265
I heard something
in the background real faint.

838
00:54:25,348 --> 00:54:27,267
After all those subs took off,

839
00:54:27,350 --> 00:54:30,770
I caught it again
and got it on tape.

840
00:54:30,854 --> 00:54:32,772
I washed it through the computer

841
00:54:32,856 --> 00:54:35,316
and was able to
isolate this sound.

842
00:54:37,360 --> 00:54:39,779
When I asked the computer
to identify it,

843
00:54:39,863 --> 00:54:42,282
what I got was
magma displacement.

844
00:54:42,365 --> 00:54:44,784
You see, sir,
the SAPS software

845
00:54:44,868 --> 00:54:47,787
was originally written
to look for seismic events.

846
00:54:47,871 --> 00:54:49,831
I think when it gets confused,

847
00:54:49,914 --> 00:54:52,125
it kind of runs home to Mama.

848
00:54:52,208 --> 00:54:53,585
I'm not following you,
Jonesy.

849
00:54:53,668 --> 00:54:55,211
Sir, I'm sorry.

850
00:54:55,253 --> 00:54:58,798
Listen to it
at 10 times speed.

851
00:55:05,930 --> 00:55:09,267
Now, that's got to
be manmade, Captain.

852
00:55:09,309 --> 00:55:10,101
All right.

853
00:55:12,437 --> 00:55:14,898
The first contact was at 09:15,

854
00:55:14,939 --> 00:55:16,858
and the bearing was 2-6-9.

855
00:55:16,941 --> 00:55:18,902
At 09:30, it was here.

856
00:55:18,985 --> 00:55:22,447
I came back to it at 11:00
and 11:15, here and here.

857
00:55:22,489 --> 00:55:24,449
Remember the dispatch we got

858
00:55:24,491 --> 00:55:25,784
about Russian sub skippers

859
00:55:25,867 --> 00:55:28,370
running the Reykjanes Ridge
at high speeds

860
00:55:28,453 --> 00:55:30,288
because they had
hyper-accurate surveys

861
00:55:30,372 --> 00:55:31,831
of the underwater canyons?

862
00:55:31,915 --> 00:55:33,792
The front door to those canyons

863
00:55:33,875 --> 00:55:36,294
was a formation
called Thor's Twins.

864
00:55:36,378 --> 00:55:37,587
Yeah?

865
00:55:50,392 --> 00:55:51,726
Look at that.

866
00:55:54,270 --> 00:55:55,855
Right into Red Route One.

867
00:55:55,939 --> 00:55:56,815
Captain.

868
00:55:58,400 --> 00:55:59,818
You may think I'm crazy,

869
00:55:59,901 --> 00:56:01,820
but I'll bet that
magma displacement

870
00:56:01,903 --> 00:56:04,322
was actually some
new Russian sub,

871
00:56:04,406 --> 00:56:06,616
and it's headed
for the lceland coast.

872
00:56:08,910 --> 00:56:11,371
Have I got this straight,
Jonesy?

873
00:56:11,454 --> 00:56:15,333
A $40 million computer tells you
you're chasing an earthquake,

874
00:56:15,417 --> 00:56:16,835
but you don't believe it,

875
00:56:16,918 --> 00:56:20,338
and you come up
with this on your own?

876
00:56:20,422 --> 00:56:21,506
Yes, sir.

877
00:56:21,589 --> 00:56:23,008
Including all the navigation math?

878
00:56:23,091 --> 00:56:24,551
Sir, I've got all the...

879
00:56:24,634 --> 00:56:26,678
Relax, Jonesy. You sold me.

880
00:56:28,096 --> 00:56:32,017
Tommy, I want you
to plot us a speed course

881
00:56:32,100 --> 00:56:35,020
for the bottom end
of Red Route One.

882
00:56:35,103 --> 00:56:37,188
We'll never find him
in those canyons.

883
00:56:37,272 --> 00:56:39,524
First take us
to periscope depth.

884
00:56:39,607 --> 00:56:40,817
This we got to phone in.

885
00:56:40,859 --> 00:56:43,028
Aye, aye, Captain.

886
00:56:51,661 --> 00:56:53,371
Hang on, Jonesy.

887
00:56:58,126 --> 00:57:00,879
If I can get you close enough,

888
00:57:00,962 --> 00:57:02,505
can you track this sucker?

889
00:57:02,547 --> 00:57:04,215
Yes, sir.

890
00:57:04,299 --> 00:57:07,218
Now that I know
what to listen for,

891
00:57:07,302 --> 00:57:08,887
I'll bag him.

892
00:57:09,971 --> 00:57:11,056
Carry on.

893
00:57:11,139 --> 00:57:12,349
Yes, sir.

894
00:57:13,933 --> 00:57:16,102
That's the craziest notion
I've ever heard...

895
00:57:16,186 --> 00:57:17,270
a serving line officer?

896
00:57:17,354 --> 00:57:19,272
An entire ballistic
missile submarine?

897
00:57:19,356 --> 00:57:20,815
They can't all want to defect.

898
00:57:20,899 --> 00:57:22,776
Even if it's only the officers,

899
00:57:22,859 --> 00:57:24,319
it's extremely
difficult to believe.

900
00:57:24,402 --> 00:57:25,779
What's his plan?

901
00:57:25,862 --> 00:57:27,030
His plan?

902
00:57:27,113 --> 00:57:29,574
Russians don't take a dump, son,

903
00:57:29,616 --> 00:57:31,493
without a plan.

904
00:57:31,576 --> 00:57:34,329
Senior captains don't start
something this dangerous

905
00:57:34,412 --> 00:57:36,081
without having thought
the matter through.

906
00:57:36,164 --> 00:57:37,248
What's he going to do,

907
00:57:37,332 --> 00:57:40,460
sail into New York, pop the hatch,
and say here I am?

908
00:57:40,543 --> 00:57:42,504
It might be just
that simple, yes.

909
00:57:42,587 --> 00:57:44,714
Things may appear simple
in the cubicle at CIA,

910
00:57:44,798 --> 00:57:47,050
but in the middle
of the Atlantic

911
00:57:47,133 --> 00:57:49,511
with Soviet warships
bearing down on us,

912
00:57:49,594 --> 00:57:50,929
they get more complex.

913
00:57:50,970 --> 00:57:51,930
Smoke?

914
00:57:52,013 --> 00:57:54,099
No, thank you.

915
00:57:54,182 --> 00:57:55,975
And I don't think your notion

916
00:57:56,059 --> 00:57:58,478
of a few days inspection's
going to wash either.

917
00:57:58,561 --> 00:58:00,980
In order to be of any value,

918
00:58:01,064 --> 00:58:02,524
you'd have to tear it apart.

919
00:58:02,607 --> 00:58:04,192
The metallurgy would
take a couple of months.

920
00:58:04,275 --> 00:58:05,527
Then we'd have to keep it.

921
00:58:05,610 --> 00:58:09,030
What will you do with the crew?

922
00:58:09,114 --> 00:58:13,368
Ones that don't defect will go back
and say we got the boat.

923
00:58:13,451 --> 00:58:15,453
Or do you plan
to eliminate them?

924
00:58:16,705 --> 00:58:18,748
We're not at war, sir.

925
00:58:18,790 --> 00:58:22,919
So you have to get them
off the boat in such a way

926
00:58:22,961 --> 00:58:24,921
that they think we don't have it,

927
00:58:24,963 --> 00:58:28,466
and they'll report that
we don't have it to their bosses.

928
00:58:28,550 --> 00:58:29,926
Otherwise, this whole business

929
00:58:29,968 --> 00:58:31,928
is just academic, right?

930
00:58:33,471 --> 00:58:36,391
Yes, sir.
I think I see your point.

931
00:58:38,018 --> 00:58:40,311
When's the last time you slept?

932
00:58:42,522 --> 00:58:43,898
A while.

933
00:58:44,983 --> 00:58:47,610
The chief outside
will fix you up.

934
00:58:47,652 --> 00:58:49,154
Thank you, sir.

935
00:58:49,195 --> 00:58:50,697
Captain.

936
00:58:55,660 --> 00:58:58,079
It'll be dawn
in a couple of hours.

937
00:58:58,163 --> 00:58:59,497
We'll be in the CIC.

938
00:58:59,581 --> 00:59:02,792
Things are liable to get
a little dicey around here.

939
00:59:04,127 --> 00:59:05,712
Yes, sir.

940
00:59:13,345 --> 00:59:15,138
You think he's crazy?

941
00:59:15,221 --> 00:59:16,389
Certifiable.

942
00:59:16,473 --> 00:59:18,099
No matter what
his credentials,

943
00:59:18,183 --> 00:59:21,061
I don't care for him
wearing the uniform.

944
00:59:21,144 --> 00:59:23,521
Did you see that ring
on his finger?

945
00:59:23,563 --> 00:59:26,524
The academy, class of '72.
A marine.

946
00:59:26,566 --> 00:59:27,650
You're kidding.

947
00:59:27,734 --> 00:59:29,194
How did you...

948
00:59:29,277 --> 00:59:30,695
Greer told me.

949
00:59:30,737 --> 00:59:32,405
Summer of his third year,

950
00:59:32,489 --> 00:59:34,866
he went down
in a chopper accident.

951
00:59:34,949 --> 00:59:36,868
Bad. Pilot and crew killed.

952
00:59:36,951 --> 00:59:39,871
That kid spent
10 months in traction

953
00:59:39,954 --> 00:59:42,916
and another year
learning to walk again.

954
00:59:42,999 --> 00:59:45,627
He did his fourth year
from the hospital.

955
00:59:46,628 --> 00:59:48,546
It's up to you, Charlie,

956
00:59:48,630 --> 00:59:52,425
but you might consider
cutting the kid a little slack.

957
00:59:52,509 --> 00:59:55,387
Ah, Russians are
going to find that sub

958
00:59:55,470 --> 00:59:58,056
before we get near it anyway.

959
01:00:19,994 --> 01:00:22,455
Passing Thor's Twins, sir.

960
01:00:22,539 --> 01:00:23,748
Very good.

961
01:00:28,753 --> 01:00:30,630
Passing the twins now,
Captain.

962
01:00:30,714 --> 01:00:32,590
Very good.
You may commence your run.

963
01:00:32,674 --> 01:00:34,592
Call me at the first turning.

964
01:00:34,676 --> 01:00:36,136
Aye, Captain.

965
01:00:36,219 --> 01:00:37,762
Mr. Kamarov.

966
01:00:38,847 --> 01:00:40,807
You may commence your run now.

967
01:00:40,890 --> 01:00:41,808
Aye, sir.

968
01:00:41,891 --> 01:00:45,353
First leg on my mark,
course 2-6-0.

969
01:00:45,437 --> 01:00:49,816
Speed, 18 knots.
Depth, 270 meters.

970
01:00:49,899 --> 01:00:51,401
5...

971
01:00:51,443 --> 01:00:52,444
4...

972
01:00:52,485 --> 01:00:53,486
3...

973
01:00:53,570 --> 01:00:54,487
2...

974
01:00:54,571 --> 01:00:55,947
1...

975
01:00:55,989 --> 01:00:57,449
Mark.

976
01:00:57,532 --> 01:00:59,993
Diving command, course 2-6-0.

977
01:01:00,076 --> 01:01:01,494
Course 2-6-0, aye.

978
01:01:01,578 --> 01:01:02,662
Speed, 18 knots.

979
01:01:02,746 --> 01:01:04,164
Speed, 18 knots, aye.

980
01:01:04,247 --> 01:01:05,665
Stop pissing, Yuri.

981
01:01:05,749 --> 01:01:08,001
Give me a stopwatch and a map,

982
01:01:08,084 --> 01:01:09,502
and I'll fly the Alps

983
01:01:09,586 --> 01:01:11,004
in a plane with no windows.

984
01:01:11,087 --> 01:01:13,673
If the map is accurate enough.

985
01:01:34,736 --> 01:01:37,655
We're in the lane
and approaching first turn.

986
01:01:37,739 --> 01:01:42,035
Come left to course 1-9-5.

987
01:01:42,118 --> 01:01:44,037
In 30 seconds,

988
01:01:44,120 --> 01:01:45,997
decrease depth to 200 meters.

989
01:01:46,081 --> 01:01:47,540
Maintain speed.

990
01:01:47,624 --> 01:01:49,167
Very good, Navigator.

991
01:01:51,086 --> 01:01:53,546
Captain, we're approaching
the first turn.

992
01:01:53,630 --> 01:01:56,007
25 seconds to course 1-9-5.

993
01:01:56,091 --> 01:01:58,927
Increase speed to 26 knots
and recompute.

994
01:02:02,097 --> 01:02:03,515
Aye, Captain.

995
01:02:05,100 --> 01:02:08,186
Navigator, recompute
for 26 knots.

996
01:02:09,646 --> 01:02:11,564
Turn on my mark.

997
01:02:11,648 --> 01:02:17,028
5... 4... 3... 2... 1...

998
01:02:17,112 --> 01:02:18,029
Mark.

999
01:02:18,113 --> 01:02:20,031
Diving control,
come left to 1-9-5.

1000
01:02:20,115 --> 01:02:21,533
Come left to 1-9-5.

1001
01:02:21,616 --> 01:02:22,826
Up on the bow planes.

1002
01:02:30,709 --> 01:02:32,502
Course now 1-9-5

1003
01:02:32,544 --> 01:02:36,006
and maintaining speed
26 knots.

1004
01:02:39,926 --> 01:02:41,886
Mr. Kamarov?

1005
01:02:41,928 --> 01:02:43,847
Next leg, Captain,

1006
01:02:43,888 --> 01:02:48,351
come right
to course 2-4-0 now at...

1007
01:02:48,393 --> 01:02:50,395
8 minutes and 40 seconds.

1008
01:02:50,437 --> 01:02:53,523
Very good.
Maintain course and speed.

1009
01:02:56,901 --> 01:02:58,862
Too fast, Vasily.

1010
01:02:58,903 --> 01:02:59,904
Too fast.

1011
01:02:59,946 --> 01:03:02,866
Those charts are
laid out precisely...

1012
01:03:02,907 --> 01:03:05,869
so many knots
for so many seconds.

1013
01:03:05,910 --> 01:03:08,371
And this thing
handles like a pig.

1014
01:03:08,413 --> 01:03:10,874
Watch your bearing,
Mr. Slavin.

1015
01:03:17,172 --> 01:03:19,382
15 seconds to turn, Captain.

1016
01:03:19,424 --> 01:03:21,051
Should we decrease speed?

1017
01:03:21,092 --> 01:03:22,093
Negative.

1018
01:03:22,135 --> 01:03:24,512
Prepare to come right.

1019
01:03:24,596 --> 01:03:25,722
Aye, sir.

1020
01:03:25,805 --> 01:03:26,765
8...

1021
01:03:26,806 --> 01:03:27,849
7...

1022
01:03:27,932 --> 01:03:28,850
6...

1023
01:03:28,933 --> 01:03:29,893
5...

1024
01:03:29,976 --> 01:03:30,935
4...

1025
01:03:30,977 --> 01:03:31,936
3...

1026
01:03:31,978 --> 01:03:32,896
2...

1027
01:03:32,937 --> 01:03:34,397
1...Mark.

1028
01:03:34,439 --> 01:03:36,941
15 degrees down angle
on the bow planes.

1029
01:03:36,983 --> 01:03:39,235
Come right 2-4-0.

1030
01:03:39,277 --> 01:03:40,570
Move it!

1031
01:04:00,006 --> 01:04:01,383
We're in the lane.

1032
01:04:01,466 --> 01:04:04,594
Next leg 34 minutes
to the Neptune Massif.

1033
01:04:04,636 --> 01:04:07,597
Course 2-4-0.

1034
01:04:20,068 --> 01:04:21,444
What happened?

1035
01:04:22,362 --> 01:04:23,780
The cryogenic plant!

1036
01:04:23,822 --> 01:04:25,281
The magnets aren't cooling.

1037
01:04:25,323 --> 01:04:26,783
Temperature in
the caterpillar

1038
01:04:26,825 --> 01:04:29,786
is 15 degrees above red line
and rising.

1039
01:04:29,828 --> 01:04:31,287
It's going to melt.

1040
01:04:31,329 --> 01:04:33,623
Kill it! Shut it down!

1041
01:04:38,044 --> 01:04:39,504
Captain?

1042
01:04:39,546 --> 01:04:40,463
What happened?

1043
01:04:40,505 --> 01:04:42,465
The cryogenic plant failed.

1044
01:04:42,507 --> 01:04:44,801
Reactor damage?

1045
01:04:44,843 --> 01:04:46,469
There was a power spike,

1046
01:04:46,511 --> 01:04:48,304
but the reactor
scrammed automatically.

1047
01:04:48,388 --> 01:04:50,390
Was there any
radiation leakage?

1048
01:04:50,473 --> 01:04:51,933
I don't know yet!

1049
01:04:52,017 --> 01:04:54,144
How long will it take to fix?

1050
01:04:54,185 --> 01:04:56,479
I got to find
what's wrong first.

1051
01:04:56,521 --> 01:04:59,649
Could be the liquid helium
or the superconductors.

1052
01:05:00,900 --> 01:05:02,485
Captain, we will have to stop

1053
01:05:02,527 --> 01:05:04,988
until we can repair
the caterpillar unit.

1054
01:05:05,030 --> 01:05:07,657
Negative.
We'll run on normal propulsion.

1055
01:05:09,075 --> 01:05:11,494
Captain,
we will not be silent.

1056
01:05:11,536 --> 01:05:13,580
Make revolutions for 20 knots

1057
01:05:13,663 --> 01:05:16,666
and engage the propellers.

1058
01:05:20,045 --> 01:05:21,546
Aye, Captain.

1059
01:05:21,588 --> 01:05:23,173
Commence engine start-up.

1060
01:05:23,214 --> 01:05:25,508
He'll get us all killed.

1061
01:05:30,555 --> 01:05:33,183
The captain knows
what he's doing.

1062
01:05:34,059 --> 01:05:35,185
Doctor.

1063
01:05:38,229 --> 01:05:40,023
Naval activities?

1064
01:05:40,106 --> 01:05:42,317
I have no knowledge of this.

1065
01:05:42,400 --> 01:05:44,778
But then,
I never was a sailor.

1066
01:05:44,819 --> 01:05:46,571
Mr. Ambassador,

1067
01:05:46,654 --> 01:05:49,074
you have nearly
100 vessels operating

1068
01:05:49,157 --> 01:05:51,159
in the North Atlantic
right now.

1069
01:05:51,242 --> 01:05:53,286
Your aircraft have dropped
enough sonar buoys

1070
01:05:53,370 --> 01:05:56,664
that a man could walk
from Greenland to Scotland

1071
01:05:56,706 --> 01:05:58,500
without getting his feet wet.

1072
01:05:58,541 --> 01:06:00,418
Shall we dispense with the bull?

1073
01:06:00,502 --> 01:06:01,920
You make your point

1074
01:06:02,003 --> 01:06:06,174
as delicately as ever,
Mr. Pelt, but...

1075
01:06:06,257 --> 01:06:08,551
And what looks like an exercise

1076
01:06:08,635 --> 01:06:10,512
could be a prelude to war.

1077
01:06:10,595 --> 01:06:11,930
How can we tell the difference?

1078
01:06:12,013 --> 01:06:15,934
Prudence demands that we
deploy ships to observe yours.

1079
01:06:16,017 --> 01:06:17,519
Your government should consider

1080
01:06:17,602 --> 01:06:21,022
that having
your ships and ours,

1081
01:06:21,106 --> 01:06:23,024
your aircraft and ours,

1082
01:06:23,108 --> 01:06:27,529
in such proximity
is inherently dangerous.

1083
01:06:27,612 --> 01:06:31,366
Wars have begun that way,
Mr. Ambassador.

1084
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:42,919
We have lost
one of our submarines.

1085
01:06:43,003 --> 01:06:43,920
Lost it?

1086
01:06:44,004 --> 01:06:46,923
We fear she may be down, and...

1087
01:06:47,007 --> 01:06:48,550
this is most embarrassing, but...

1088
01:06:48,633 --> 01:06:52,053
several of the officers
are sons of...

1089
01:06:52,137 --> 01:06:55,473
high party officials.

1090
01:06:55,557 --> 01:06:57,475
One is even the son

1091
01:06:57,559 --> 01:06:59,477
of a Central Committee member.

1092
01:06:59,561 --> 01:07:01,396
I cannot say which.

1093
01:07:01,479 --> 01:07:05,608
You're telling me this is
a massive rescue operation?

1094
01:07:05,692 --> 01:07:07,694
That is correct.

1095
01:07:09,654 --> 01:07:12,615
I'm terribly sorry.

1096
01:07:12,699 --> 01:07:14,451
How can we help?

1097
01:07:16,703 --> 01:07:18,329
I'm not sure that...

1098
01:07:18,413 --> 01:07:21,332
Perhaps a joint rescue mission?

1099
01:07:24,169 --> 01:07:27,130
That is very gracious of you, sir.

1100
01:07:27,213 --> 01:07:30,091
I'll pass your offer on.

1101
01:07:30,175 --> 01:07:31,593
But at this time,

1102
01:07:31,676 --> 01:07:33,595
I think we're doing everything

1103
01:07:33,678 --> 01:07:35,347
that can be done.

1104
01:07:53,239 --> 01:07:54,866
Captain?

1105
01:07:58,453 --> 01:08:01,289
Our strategy depends
on your answer.

1106
01:08:01,331 --> 01:08:04,292
The entire fleet
will know where we are.

1107
01:08:04,334 --> 01:08:05,460
Captain, Sonar.

1108
01:08:05,543 --> 01:08:08,797
We've been overflown
by a multi-engine turboprop.

1109
01:08:08,880 --> 01:08:10,924
Put it on audio.

1110
01:08:14,344 --> 01:08:16,262
Short transients,
close aboard.

1111
01:08:17,514 --> 01:08:19,474
Water entry of small objects.

1112
01:08:19,557 --> 01:08:21,309
Sonar buoys.

1113
01:08:22,727 --> 01:08:23,645
Battle stations.

1114
01:08:23,728 --> 01:08:25,313
Battle stations!

1115
01:08:25,397 --> 01:08:26,314
Battle stations!

1116
01:08:26,398 --> 01:08:27,315
Battle stations!

1117
01:08:27,399 --> 01:08:29,442
How long to Neptune Massif?

1118
01:08:31,444 --> 01:08:32,821
How long?

1119
01:08:32,904 --> 01:08:36,241
4 minutes, 10 seconds.

1120
01:08:36,324 --> 01:08:37,826
Should we bottom the boat?

1121
01:08:37,909 --> 01:08:39,369
No. Too late now.

1122
01:08:39,452 --> 01:08:42,163
We've run out of time.

1123
01:08:42,247 --> 01:08:43,248
Countermeasures.

1124
01:08:43,331 --> 01:08:44,708
Prepare to launch missiles.

1125
01:08:44,791 --> 01:08:46,251
Aye, sir.

1126
01:08:54,676 --> 01:08:56,177
Aircraft on computer guidance.

1127
01:08:56,219 --> 01:08:57,846
Request permission to launch.

1128
01:08:57,929 --> 01:09:01,057
You are authorized
to launch the weapon.

1129
01:09:03,309 --> 01:09:05,478
Weapon is away.

1130
01:09:09,190 --> 01:09:10,650
High speed screw!

1131
01:09:10,692 --> 01:09:12,318
Torpedo in the water!

1132
01:09:12,360 --> 01:09:13,903
Stand by.

1133
01:09:15,238 --> 01:09:16,740
Torpedo is active.

1134
01:09:19,200 --> 01:09:21,327
Torpedo has acquired.

1135
01:09:21,369 --> 01:09:23,121
Launch countermeasures.

1136
01:09:23,204 --> 01:09:24,831
Launch countermeasures.
Aye, sir.

1137
01:09:31,171 --> 01:09:32,547
We're in the lane.

1138
01:09:32,630 --> 01:09:35,508
How long till
the turn at Massif?

1139
01:09:35,550 --> 01:09:36,676
2 minutes, 9 seconds.

1140
01:09:36,718 --> 01:09:39,846
We have no room to maneuver
in these canyons.

1141
01:09:39,888 --> 01:09:41,348
Shut up.

1142
01:09:46,269 --> 01:09:48,688
Torpedo has reached
countermeasures.

1143
01:09:48,730 --> 01:09:50,190
Torpedo has lost contact.

1144
01:09:50,231 --> 01:09:52,359
Massif approaching.

1145
01:09:55,070 --> 01:09:57,364
Torpedo has reacquired.

1146
01:09:57,405 --> 01:10:01,242
Torpedo has reacquired
and is homing.

1147
01:10:01,284 --> 01:10:02,744
Give me the count.

1148
01:10:02,786 --> 01:10:06,206
Turn at Massif 40 degrees starboard
in 30 seconds.

1149
01:10:06,247 --> 01:10:08,291
Time to torpedo impact?

1150
01:10:08,375 --> 01:10:10,418
Torpedo impact, 35 seconds.

1151
01:10:10,502 --> 01:10:12,170
Increase to flank.

1152
01:10:12,253 --> 01:10:13,213
Full ahead flank.

1153
01:10:13,254 --> 01:10:14,381
Full ahead flank.

1154
01:10:14,464 --> 01:10:15,715
Full ahead flank, aye.

1155
01:10:15,757 --> 01:10:18,093
Torpedo impact now 40 seconds.

1156
01:10:19,427 --> 01:10:21,346
35 seconds.

1157
01:10:28,853 --> 01:10:30,397
30 seconds.

1158
01:10:31,314 --> 01:10:33,274
Turn at Massif in...

1159
01:10:33,358 --> 01:10:35,276
7...

1160
01:10:35,318 --> 01:10:36,778
6...

1161
01:10:36,820 --> 01:10:38,279
5...

1162
01:10:38,321 --> 01:10:39,280
4...

1163
01:10:39,322 --> 01:10:40,782
3...

1164
01:10:40,824 --> 01:10:41,908
2...

1165
01:10:41,991 --> 01:10:42,909
1...

1166
01:10:42,951 --> 01:10:43,910
Mark.

1167
01:10:43,952 --> 01:10:45,578
Torpedo impact 25 seconds.

1168
01:10:45,662 --> 01:10:46,746
The turn, Captain.

1169
01:10:46,830 --> 01:10:47,831
Not yet.

1170
01:10:50,959 --> 01:10:53,044
Mark plus 8 seconds...

1171
01:10:53,128 --> 01:10:54,129
plus 9...

1172
01:10:54,212 --> 01:10:55,463
plus 10 seconds...

1173
01:10:55,505 --> 01:10:57,632
plus 11...plus 12...

1174
01:10:57,716 --> 01:10:59,134
plus 13 seconds...

1175
01:10:59,217 --> 01:11:00,135
plus 14...

1176
01:11:00,218 --> 01:11:01,636
plus 15 seconds.

1177
01:11:01,678 --> 01:11:05,640
Captain, we're out of position
by a boat length.

1178
01:11:05,682 --> 01:11:08,184
Torpedo impact now 15 seconds.

1179
01:11:08,268 --> 01:11:10,437
Sound collision.

1180
01:11:10,478 --> 01:11:11,563
Sound collision.

1181
01:11:11,646 --> 01:11:13,356
We're out of the lane!

1182
01:11:13,440 --> 01:11:14,899
You're relieved.

1183
01:11:14,983 --> 01:11:16,317
Borodin?

1184
01:11:22,073 --> 01:11:23,366
Right full rudder!

1185
01:11:23,450 --> 01:11:24,451
Reverse starboard engine!

1186
01:11:24,492 --> 01:11:25,452
Right full rudder.

1187
01:11:25,493 --> 01:11:26,661
All back starboard shaft.

1188
01:11:26,745 --> 01:11:28,455
All back starboard shaft!

1189
01:11:31,499 --> 01:11:34,127
Torpedo impact 12 seconds...

1190
01:11:34,210 --> 01:11:37,172
10... 9... 8...

1191
01:11:37,255 --> 01:11:41,301
7... 6... 5...

1192
01:12:11,289 --> 01:12:13,875
Captain, they're
really shooting at us!

1193
01:12:13,958 --> 01:12:14,876
Pull yourself together!

1194
01:12:14,959 --> 01:12:17,504
Boy! Easy, boy.

1195
01:12:17,545 --> 01:12:20,674
If they were really
shooting at us,

1196
01:12:20,715 --> 01:12:22,676
we'd be dead by now.

1197
01:12:22,717 --> 01:12:24,678
Give me some line.

1198
01:12:41,569 --> 01:12:44,614
I found out what happened
to the caterpillar.

1199
01:12:44,698 --> 01:12:48,076
The buffer circuit
has been torn out.

1200
01:12:48,159 --> 01:12:51,079
It was only a matter of time

1201
01:12:51,162 --> 01:12:53,415
before an overload
shut it down.

1202
01:12:53,456 --> 01:12:55,083
What are you saying?

1203
01:12:55,166 --> 01:12:58,086
Whoever did this knew how
to cripple the caterpillar

1204
01:12:58,169 --> 01:13:00,130
in a way not easy to find.

1205
01:13:00,213 --> 01:13:03,258
Captain,
we have a saboteur on board.

1206
01:13:07,178 --> 01:13:10,098
It can't be any of the officers.

1207
01:13:10,181 --> 01:13:13,143
They had plenty of time
before we embarked.

1208
01:13:13,226 --> 01:13:15,145
Must be a crew member.

1209
01:13:15,228 --> 01:13:16,604
Go to Putin's cabin.

1210
01:13:16,688 --> 01:13:19,607
Check his files
for information on the crew.

1211
01:13:19,691 --> 01:13:23,737
We may have to put them off
before we planned.

1212
01:13:25,697 --> 01:13:28,700
The average Russky, son,
don't take a dump

1213
01:13:28,783 --> 01:13:30,285
without a plan.

1214
01:13:32,245 --> 01:13:33,788
Wait a minute.

1215
01:13:35,206 --> 01:13:38,626
We don't have to get
the crew off the sub.

1216
01:13:38,710 --> 01:13:41,171
He would have had to do that.

1217
01:13:41,254 --> 01:13:45,633
We just have to figure out
what he's going to do.

1218
01:13:45,717 --> 01:13:48,595
How is he going
to get them off the sub?

1219
01:13:49,721 --> 01:13:53,641
They'd have to want to get off.

1220
01:13:53,725 --> 01:13:56,186
How do you get a crew

1221
01:13:56,269 --> 01:13:59,773
to want to get off a submarine?

1222
01:13:59,814 --> 01:14:01,816
How do you get them
to want to get off

1223
01:14:01,858 --> 01:14:03,401
a nuclear sub...

1224
01:14:03,485 --> 01:14:06,112
I know how he's going
to evacuate the sub.

1225
01:14:06,196 --> 01:14:07,614
Not now, Jack.

1226
01:14:07,655 --> 01:14:10,617
Yankee One star base,
say again status.

1227
01:14:10,658 --> 01:14:12,118
Declare an emergency.

1228
01:14:12,160 --> 01:14:14,120
Declare an emergency.

1229
01:14:14,162 --> 01:14:15,663
Yankee One,
requesting vectors.

1230
01:14:15,705 --> 01:14:18,625
Vector 2-7-0 speed is buster.

1231
01:14:18,667 --> 01:14:21,127
Yankee One's
losing hydraulic pressure.

1232
01:14:21,169 --> 01:14:22,879
Say again, Yankee One.

1233
01:14:22,962 --> 01:14:25,507
Bear Foxtrot got too close
to the group.

1234
01:14:25,590 --> 01:14:29,135
One of the F-14s crowded him,
and they bumped.

1235
01:14:29,219 --> 01:14:30,679
He's losing hydraulic fluid.

1236
01:14:30,720 --> 01:14:34,015
They're trying to get him
back aboard now.

1237
01:14:34,057 --> 01:14:35,517
Busy morning.

1238
01:14:35,600 --> 01:14:37,227
Hmm.

1239
01:14:37,310 --> 01:14:42,524
Russian attack subs are now stationed
off every East Coast port.

1240
01:14:42,607 --> 01:14:44,526
We're up here.

1241
01:14:44,567 --> 01:14:47,529
The New Jersey
is moving up the coast

1242
01:14:47,570 --> 01:14:51,032
while the Russians,
aside from over 100 bear foxtrots,

1243
01:14:51,074 --> 01:14:54,536
have a row of subs
and several surface groups.

1244
01:14:54,577 --> 01:14:55,995
That's a lot of firepower.

1245
01:14:56,037 --> 01:14:57,914
For a rescue team, yes.

1246
01:14:57,997 --> 01:14:59,416
There's something else strange.

1247
01:14:59,499 --> 01:15:02,502
They're using sonar as if
they're looking for something,

1248
01:15:02,585 --> 01:15:04,045
but nobody's listening.

1249
01:15:04,087 --> 01:15:05,547
What do you mean?

1250
01:15:05,588 --> 01:15:09,217
At their speed they could
run over my daughter's stereo

1251
01:15:09,300 --> 01:15:10,802
and not hear it.

1252
01:15:11,803 --> 01:15:13,722
They're not searching
for Ramius.

1253
01:15:13,805 --> 01:15:15,223
They're driving him.

1254
01:15:15,306 --> 01:15:16,850
Driving him where?

1255
01:15:20,603 --> 01:15:23,023
The hounds to the hunters.

1256
01:15:23,064 --> 01:15:26,067
Your sub captain's going
to make it to America.

1257
01:15:26,109 --> 01:15:29,571
He's going to die
within sight of it.

1258
01:15:29,612 --> 01:15:33,575
His wing man kept requesting
permission to fire.

1259
01:15:33,616 --> 01:15:36,995
Somebody messes up, we'll be
in the biggest naval battle

1260
01:15:37,078 --> 01:15:39,497
since the Jutland.
Jesus!

1261
01:15:40,623 --> 01:15:43,084
Water's too cold to eject him.

1262
01:15:43,168 --> 01:15:45,712
Air boss will try
to bring him aboard.

1263
01:15:45,795 --> 01:15:48,590
Sir, what's this sub
off by herself?

1264
01:15:48,673 --> 01:15:49,924
Bart Mancuso's boat.

1265
01:15:49,966 --> 01:15:53,428
He's going to the bottom
of Red Route One.

1266
01:15:53,511 --> 01:15:54,929
He's had intermittent contact

1267
01:15:54,971 --> 01:15:58,391
with a magma displacement.
He's got this...

1268
01:15:58,433 --> 01:15:59,434
Magma displacement?

1269
01:15:59,476 --> 01:16:01,603
Is that like a seismic anomaly?

1270
01:16:01,686 --> 01:16:03,605
I suppose so. Why?

1271
01:16:03,646 --> 01:16:07,442
Is there a way you could
get me onboard the Dallas?

1272
01:16:07,484 --> 01:16:08,818
What for?

1273
01:16:08,902 --> 01:16:11,363
I think Mancuso's found
the Red October.

1274
01:16:11,446 --> 01:16:13,323
He hasn't found anything, Jack.

1275
01:16:13,406 --> 01:16:14,824
He's just waiting.

1276
01:16:14,908 --> 01:16:16,785
Sir, this silent propulsion system

1277
01:16:16,868 --> 01:16:19,371
could easily be mistaken
for something...

1278
01:16:19,454 --> 01:16:23,083
The only way to get you
that far north

1279
01:16:23,124 --> 01:16:27,504
is to use a chopper turned
into a flying gas can.

1280
01:16:27,587 --> 01:16:29,964
Admiral, Yankee One
is on final.

1281
01:16:30,048 --> 01:16:31,966
Rescue personnel
are in place.

1282
01:16:32,050 --> 01:16:33,968
Pick up your left wing.

1283
01:16:34,052 --> 01:16:35,470
You're drifting left.

1284
01:16:35,553 --> 01:16:36,971
Power, power!

1285
01:16:37,055 --> 01:16:38,807
Wave off, wave off!

1286
01:16:38,848 --> 01:16:40,975
Eject, eject, eject!

1287
01:16:51,069 --> 01:16:52,028
Fire, fire!

1288
01:16:52,112 --> 01:16:54,698
Fire on the flight deck!

1289
01:16:54,781 --> 01:16:57,701
This business will
get out of control,

1290
01:16:57,784 --> 01:17:00,870
and we'll be lucky
to live through it.

1291
01:17:02,622 --> 01:17:04,499
Fire on the flight deck.

1292
01:17:04,582 --> 01:17:06,668
All hands lend assistance.

1293
01:17:12,590 --> 01:17:15,510
The bird's loaded down
with enough fuel to get you there,

1294
01:17:15,593 --> 01:17:17,595
but Dallas may not be there.

1295
01:17:17,679 --> 01:17:19,347
We're trying to reach her.

1296
01:17:19,431 --> 01:17:21,099
If she's gone deep,
it's dodgey.

1297
01:17:21,182 --> 01:17:23,727
If you have to ditch...

1298
01:17:30,608 --> 01:17:32,569
If you have to ditch,

1299
01:17:32,652 --> 01:17:35,280
don't think about anything
but the survival gear.

1300
01:17:35,363 --> 01:17:36,698
At this water's temperature,

1301
01:17:36,781 --> 01:17:38,533
you'll have about four minutes.

1302
01:17:38,616 --> 01:17:40,535
I'll try to remember that.

1303
01:17:43,830 --> 01:17:47,208
Next time, Jack,
write a goddamn memo.

1304
01:18:12,108 --> 01:18:15,070
Diving Officer,
make your depth 6-5-0 feet.

1305
01:18:15,153 --> 01:18:17,572
Make my depth 6-5-0 feet.

1306
01:18:17,655 --> 01:18:20,575
Sonar, Conn preceding
below the thermal layer.

1307
01:18:20,658 --> 01:18:22,077
The captain's sacked out?

1308
01:18:22,160 --> 01:18:23,078
Yeah.

1309
01:18:23,161 --> 01:18:26,081
Auxiliaries
proceed 5,000 pounds.

1310
01:18:26,164 --> 01:18:27,582
What about him?

1311
01:18:27,665 --> 01:18:29,125
He won't leave.

1312
01:18:29,209 --> 01:18:30,543
Conn. Sonar, hold.

1313
01:18:30,585 --> 01:18:34,422
No contacts on bearing 2-9-5.

1314
01:18:38,802 --> 01:18:40,261
You know...

1315
01:18:40,303 --> 01:18:43,098
I seen me a mermaid once.

1316
01:18:43,139 --> 01:18:47,060
I've even seen me
a shark eat a octopus,

1317
01:18:47,102 --> 01:18:50,563
but I ain't never seen
no phantom Russian submarine.

1318
01:19:28,226 --> 01:19:30,645
We're emerging
from the route, Captain.

1319
01:19:30,687 --> 01:19:33,148
I've ordered a routine clearing
of the baffles.

1320
01:19:33,231 --> 01:19:36,985
We'll be making the first turn
in a few minutes.

1321
01:19:37,944 --> 01:19:39,487
The caterpillar is operational

1322
01:19:39,571 --> 01:19:42,657
and appears to be
running normally.

1323
01:19:44,409 --> 01:19:47,537
The crew know about the saboteur.

1324
01:19:47,620 --> 01:19:49,497
They are afraid.

1325
01:19:50,957 --> 01:19:54,127
Well, that could be useful
when the time comes.

1326
01:19:57,255 --> 01:20:01,676
You know, we could make
the Labrador Coast in 16 hours.

1327
01:20:01,760 --> 01:20:06,181
Half of them would freeze
before they were rescued.

1328
01:20:06,264 --> 01:20:09,225
No, it's Massachusetts
or Maine...

1329
01:20:09,309 --> 01:20:11,144
in 48 hours.

1330
01:20:11,186 --> 01:20:13,229
Two days.

1331
01:20:20,195 --> 01:20:23,948
Do you think they will
let me live in Montana?

1332
01:20:31,498 --> 01:20:36,628
I would think they'll
let you live wherever you want.

1333
01:20:37,671 --> 01:20:39,089
Good.

1334
01:20:39,172 --> 01:20:42,133
Then I will live in Montana,

1335
01:20:42,217 --> 01:20:45,136
and I will marry
a round American woman

1336
01:20:45,220 --> 01:20:46,763
and raise rabbits,

1337
01:20:46,846 --> 01:20:50,100
and she will cook them for me,

1338
01:20:50,183 --> 01:20:53,269
and I will have a pickup truck...

1339
01:20:54,521 --> 01:20:55,772
or a...

1340
01:20:57,023 --> 01:21:01,111
possibly even
a recreational vehicle

1341
01:21:01,194 --> 01:21:05,323
and drive from state to state.

1342
01:21:06,199 --> 01:21:08,660
Do they let you do that?

1343
01:21:08,743 --> 01:21:09,661
Yes.

1344
01:21:09,744 --> 01:21:11,037
No papers?

1345
01:21:11,079 --> 01:21:12,622
No papers.

1346
01:21:12,706 --> 01:21:14,374
State to state.

1347
01:21:14,416 --> 01:21:16,584
All right.
Commence your turn.

1348
01:21:20,338 --> 01:21:21,923
Well, then...

1349
01:21:22,007 --> 01:21:26,094
in winter I will live
in Arizona.

1350
01:21:27,345 --> 01:21:30,598
Actually, I think
I will need two wives.

1351
01:21:30,640 --> 01:21:32,809
Oh, at least.

1352
01:21:41,901 --> 01:21:44,988
Possible aspect change
on target.

1353
01:21:45,071 --> 01:21:46,489
Sonar, Conn. Aye.

1354
01:21:46,573 --> 01:21:49,617
Concur possible target zig
based on bearing rate.

1355
01:21:52,662 --> 01:21:54,247
Conn! Sonar! Crazy lvan!

1356
01:21:54,289 --> 01:21:55,790
All stop! Quick quiet!

1357
01:21:55,832 --> 01:21:57,250
All stop, aye.

1358
01:21:57,292 --> 01:21:58,918
Engine's all stopped.

1359
01:22:04,466 --> 01:22:06,259
What's going on, Jonesy?

1360
01:22:06,301 --> 01:22:08,636
Russian captains
sometimes turn suddenly

1361
01:22:08,720 --> 01:22:11,639
to see if anyone's behind them.

1362
01:22:11,723 --> 01:22:13,641
We call it Crazy lvan.

1363
01:22:13,725 --> 01:22:16,644
The only thing
you can do is go dead,

1364
01:22:16,686 --> 01:22:20,273
shut everything down, and make
like a hole in the water.

1365
01:22:20,315 --> 01:22:21,941
So what's the catch?

1366
01:22:22,025 --> 01:22:24,110
Catch is a boat this big

1367
01:22:24,194 --> 01:22:26,154
doesn't exactly stop on a dime.

1368
01:22:26,196 --> 01:22:27,655
If we're too close,

1369
01:22:27,697 --> 01:22:30,241
we'll drift right
into the back of him.

1370
01:22:33,536 --> 01:22:37,332
What about you?
What do you look forward to?

1371
01:22:47,592 --> 01:22:49,135
I...

1372
01:22:49,219 --> 01:22:51,680
I have no such appetites.

1373
01:22:53,056 --> 01:22:56,351
Now, let's see if he can hear us.

1374
01:23:01,231 --> 01:23:04,150
Well, there must be something.

1375
01:23:11,574 --> 01:23:13,159
What is it?

1376
01:23:14,119 --> 01:23:15,870
Ahh.

1377
01:23:17,247 --> 01:23:21,209
I miss...
the peace of fishing

1378
01:23:21,251 --> 01:23:24,546
Iike when I was a boy.

1379
01:23:27,257 --> 01:23:30,635
40 years I have been at sea.

1380
01:23:31,553 --> 01:23:34,055
A war at sea.

1381
01:23:35,724 --> 01:23:38,643
A war with no battles.

1382
01:23:40,228 --> 01:23:41,813
No monuments.

1383
01:23:43,898 --> 01:23:45,650
Only casualties.

1384
01:23:49,571 --> 01:23:52,657
I widowed her
the day I married her.

1385
01:23:56,077 --> 01:24:00,540
My wife died while I
was at sea, you know.

1386
01:24:19,100 --> 01:24:21,519
Post guards
in the engineering spaces.

1387
01:24:21,603 --> 01:24:24,022
If he can get
to the caterpillar,

1388
01:24:24,105 --> 01:24:27,317
he can get to somewhere
more vital.

1389
01:24:29,611 --> 01:24:31,654
Where is he, Jonesy?

1390
01:24:31,738 --> 01:24:32,864
Conn, Sonar.

1391
01:24:32,947 --> 01:24:36,076
Target is now coming out
of our starboard baffles.

1392
01:24:36,159 --> 01:24:39,496
My read is that he
is returning to base course.

1393
01:24:39,579 --> 01:24:42,957
Concur. He's returning
to course 2-1-0.

1394
01:24:43,041 --> 01:24:45,627
He went right around us.

1395
01:24:45,710 --> 01:24:48,129
We'll give him
another 30 seconds,

1396
01:24:48,213 --> 01:24:50,965
then secure from silent running.

1397
01:24:51,925 --> 01:24:52,967
Captain?

1398
01:24:54,052 --> 01:24:57,138
Here's what we got
on the E.L.F.

1399
01:24:58,056 --> 01:24:58,973
Thanks.

1400
01:24:59,057 --> 01:25:00,308
Aye, sir.

1401
01:25:07,732 --> 01:25:09,317
What is it?

1402
01:25:11,778 --> 01:25:15,156
You're not going
to believe this.

1403
01:25:27,293 --> 01:25:29,254
Commander,
we are approaching no return.

1404
01:25:29,337 --> 01:25:31,131
Dallas apparently
hasn't gotten the message.

1405
01:25:31,172 --> 01:25:33,591
We have to turn back
for the carrier.

1406
01:25:33,675 --> 01:25:35,427
Give it a couple more minutes.

1407
01:25:35,510 --> 01:25:37,762
Negative. Fuel status
says we turn around now.

1408
01:25:37,846 --> 01:25:40,306
Fuel status?
You got a reserve, don't you?

1409
01:25:40,348 --> 01:25:42,100
I've got a 10-minute reserve,

1410
01:25:42,183 --> 01:25:45,020
but I'm not allowed to invade that
except during war.

1411
01:25:45,103 --> 01:25:47,355
If you don't get me
on that submarine,

1412
01:25:47,397 --> 01:25:48,606
you might have it.

1413
01:25:48,690 --> 01:25:50,859
You got 10 minutes
worth of fuel.

1414
01:25:50,942 --> 01:25:53,361
We stay here 10 more minutes.

1415
01:25:57,866 --> 01:25:59,451
Yo! Mount up!

1416
01:25:59,534 --> 01:26:01,661
Yo, I got a submarine
at around 3:00.

1417
01:26:01,703 --> 01:26:03,204
About 2 miles.

1418
01:26:05,206 --> 01:26:07,042
OK, Tony, hook him up.

1419
01:26:07,083 --> 01:26:10,253
I'll be lowering you down
to the submarine now.

1420
01:26:10,337 --> 01:26:11,713
Give me eight minutes.

1421
01:26:11,796 --> 01:26:15,216
Let's hope this thing
runs on fumes.

1422
01:26:15,300 --> 01:26:17,344
Very well.
Surface the ship.

1423
01:26:17,385 --> 01:26:18,803
Whoever this guy is,

1424
01:26:18,887 --> 01:26:21,222
he's in for one hell of a ride.

1425
01:26:21,264 --> 01:26:24,976
OK, Commander,
go ahead and sit down.

1426
01:26:25,060 --> 01:26:26,978
You ever do this before?

1427
01:26:27,062 --> 01:26:29,314
Once. On a calm day...
off Hawaii.

1428
01:26:29,397 --> 01:26:32,150
The pilot will have
a difficult time keeping steady.

1429
01:26:32,233 --> 01:26:33,651
Don't take chances
getting grounded.

1430
01:26:33,735 --> 01:26:35,653
Keep that rod in front of you.

1431
01:26:35,737 --> 01:26:36,988
In air like this,

1432
01:26:37,072 --> 01:26:39,199
the rotors will put out
enough static electricity

1433
01:26:39,240 --> 01:26:40,533
to light up Chicago.

1434
01:26:40,575 --> 01:26:42,035
That's why I joined the Navy.

1435
01:26:42,077 --> 01:26:43,870
A diver's in the escape trunk

1436
01:26:43,912 --> 01:26:46,164
in case this guy
goes in the drink.

1437
01:26:46,247 --> 01:26:48,583
Someone must have
a burr up his ass

1438
01:26:48,625 --> 01:26:50,543
to go for a stunt like this.

1439
01:27:08,937 --> 01:27:11,022
All right. Ready! Ready!

1440
01:27:11,106 --> 01:27:13,566
Get your hook up!

1441
01:27:13,650 --> 01:27:16,695
Commander, if I got
to bring you back up,

1442
01:27:16,778 --> 01:27:18,780
I'm going to shake the wire.

1443
01:27:18,863 --> 01:27:21,908
I'll give you a thumbs up,
like this.

1444
01:27:21,991 --> 01:27:23,993
Remember to cross your arms.

1445
01:27:24,077 --> 01:27:27,205
Got it? OK. Out you go.
Passenger's out the door.

1446
01:27:34,129 --> 01:27:36,923
Passenger's halfway down,
drifting forward.

1447
01:27:36,965 --> 01:27:38,091
Start forward.

1448
01:27:42,220 --> 01:27:45,557
All right, Tommy.
Get the hook on his cable.

1449
01:27:45,640 --> 01:27:47,559
On his cable. Now, watch it.

1450
01:27:47,642 --> 01:27:49,894
Don't touch him.
OK, here he comes.

1451
01:27:54,983 --> 01:27:56,609
Oh, no!

1452
01:27:56,693 --> 01:27:59,320
It would be easier
without that bloody crosswind.

1453
01:27:59,404 --> 01:28:01,322
Where is he?
I can't see him.

1454
01:28:03,491 --> 01:28:04,743
Don't touch him!

1455
01:28:06,161 --> 01:28:08,288
Jack! Jack! Jack!

1456
01:28:10,165 --> 01:28:12,167
This is never going to work.

1457
01:28:12,250 --> 01:28:13,835
OK, reel him back in.

1458
01:28:22,052 --> 01:28:24,012
Tony, what's going on back...

1459
01:28:28,516 --> 01:28:31,436
What happened? Dallas,
have you got a swimmer standing?

1460
01:28:31,519 --> 01:28:32,979
Emergency stop! Man overboard!

1461
01:28:33,021 --> 01:28:36,149
Port side!
Send out the diver!

1462
01:28:36,232 --> 01:28:38,818
Very well, Dallas.
We are history.

1463
01:28:47,702 --> 01:28:49,454
Get the doc down here!

1464
01:28:49,537 --> 01:28:51,998
I'll be in the escape trunk.

1465
01:28:54,876 --> 01:28:58,463
Down ladder! Down ladder!
Make a hole!

1466
01:29:01,383 --> 01:29:03,843
Watch his head.
Watch his head.

1467
01:29:14,562 --> 01:29:16,481
How do you do, Captain?

1468
01:29:16,564 --> 01:29:18,650
It's a pleasure to be aboard.

1469
01:29:21,736 --> 01:29:24,239
I have to talk to the President.

1470
01:29:25,407 --> 01:29:29,202
Of course.
May I inquire as to the subject?

1471
01:29:30,495 --> 01:29:32,997
It seems that the initial reports

1472
01:29:33,081 --> 01:29:36,042
that one of our submarines
was missing

1473
01:29:36,084 --> 01:29:37,585
were not completely accurate.

1474
01:29:39,504 --> 01:29:41,423
The submarine in question

1475
01:29:41,506 --> 01:29:44,926
is commanded
by Captain Marko Ramius.

1476
01:29:44,968 --> 01:29:47,137
Apparently, he has suffered

1477
01:29:47,220 --> 01:29:50,598
a kind of mental
nervous breakdown.

1478
01:29:52,017 --> 01:29:54,144
Just before he sailed,

1479
01:29:54,227 --> 01:29:57,605
he posted a letter
to Admiral Yuri Padorin

1480
01:29:57,689 --> 01:30:01,109
in which he announced
his intention to...

1481
01:30:03,194 --> 01:30:06,573
to fire his missiles
on the United States.

1482
01:30:06,614 --> 01:30:10,410
Why didn't you tell me this
the last time we met?

1483
01:30:10,452 --> 01:30:12,579
In my position
I'm sometimes compromised

1484
01:30:12,620 --> 01:30:15,790
by the fact Moscow doesn't
always tell me everything.

1485
01:30:15,874 --> 01:30:19,252
So one of your submarine captains
has gone insane?

1486
01:30:19,294 --> 01:30:21,588
What do you want from us?

1487
01:30:21,629 --> 01:30:23,089
You offered your assistance.

1488
01:30:23,131 --> 01:30:24,591
That was a rescue mission.

1489
01:30:24,632 --> 01:30:26,593
Now you want us to help you

1490
01:30:26,634 --> 01:30:28,636
hunt him down and kill him.

1491
01:30:28,678 --> 01:30:30,096
I have been instructed

1492
01:30:30,138 --> 01:30:33,391
to ask your President
for precisely that.

1493
01:30:34,351 --> 01:30:36,728
Find him?
We already found him.

1494
01:30:36,811 --> 01:30:39,105
We had to break off
to pick you up.

1495
01:30:39,147 --> 01:30:40,648
Captain, excuse me.
Coffee?

1496
01:30:40,690 --> 01:30:41,649
Thank you.

1497
01:30:41,691 --> 01:30:42,650
Cigarette?

1498
01:30:42,692 --> 01:30:43,610
Don't smoke.

1499
01:30:43,651 --> 01:30:45,570
Assuming we can find him,

1500
01:30:45,653 --> 01:30:49,115
just what exactly
am I supposed to do about it?

1501
01:30:49,157 --> 01:30:50,200
Captain, radio.

1502
01:30:50,283 --> 01:30:51,618
We're receiving
flash traffic

1503
01:30:51,659 --> 01:30:53,661
on the V.L.F.
emergency circuit.

1504
01:30:53,703 --> 01:30:55,789
Very well. Bring it down.

1505
01:30:57,165 --> 01:30:58,958
Well, Mr. Ryan?

1506
01:31:01,670 --> 01:31:04,130
Chief, excuse us for a second.

1507
01:31:04,172 --> 01:31:06,800
Leonard,
come on out of there.

1508
01:31:12,222 --> 01:31:15,684
The sub we're after
is called the Red October.

1509
01:31:15,767 --> 01:31:18,478
She's under the command
of a man named Ramius.

1510
01:31:18,520 --> 01:31:19,813
Now, there's the possibility

1511
01:31:19,854 --> 01:31:22,691
that he and a good
portion of his crew

1512
01:31:22,732 --> 01:31:24,317
are attempting to defect.

1513
01:31:26,695 --> 01:31:27,987
Defect?

1514
01:31:29,739 --> 01:31:30,990
Excuse me.

1515
01:31:47,215 --> 01:31:50,176
You say the boat's
called the Red October?

1516
01:31:50,218 --> 01:31:51,177
That's right.

1517
01:31:51,219 --> 01:31:52,220
Skipper's Ramius?

1518
01:31:52,262 --> 01:31:53,471
Right.

1519
01:31:54,556 --> 01:31:56,016
It seems the circumstances

1520
01:31:56,057 --> 01:31:58,184
have changed somewhat,
Mr. Ryan.

1521
01:32:04,733 --> 01:32:07,068
Get this man some dry clothes.

1522
01:32:07,110 --> 01:32:08,903
I'll be in the conn.

1523
01:32:08,945 --> 01:32:10,530
Aye, aye, sir.

1524
01:32:10,572 --> 01:32:12,198
"National Command Authority

1525
01:32:12,240 --> 01:32:14,868
informed Soviet typhoon class
submarine Red October

1526
01:32:14,951 --> 01:32:16,411
is potential renegade

1527
01:32:16,494 --> 01:32:18,371
and threatens
independent missile launch.

1528
01:32:18,413 --> 01:32:21,374
You are authorized
to use any necessary force

1529
01:32:21,416 --> 01:32:22,876
to prevent said submarine

1530
01:32:22,917 --> 01:32:23,877
from approaching

1531
01:32:23,918 --> 01:32:26,046
the coast
of the United States".

1532
01:32:40,268 --> 01:32:43,730
Left 10 degrees rudder
steady on course 2-6-5.

1533
01:32:43,772 --> 01:32:47,817
Left 10 degrees rudder
secure course of 2-6-5.

1534
01:32:47,901 --> 01:32:49,736
Make turns for 14 knots.

1535
01:32:49,778 --> 01:32:52,572
Chief of the watch,
rig for red.

1536
01:32:55,116 --> 01:32:56,409
Time Quartermasters
and Navigator

1537
01:32:56,451 --> 01:32:59,079
on the deck in the conn.

1538
01:33:02,207 --> 01:33:03,541
What's going on?

1539
01:33:03,625 --> 01:33:06,086
Battle stations
are manned and ready.

1540
01:33:06,127 --> 01:33:07,045
Very well.

1541
01:33:07,128 --> 01:33:09,589
Captain, I have to talk to you.

1542
01:33:09,631 --> 01:33:10,382
Not now.

1543
01:33:10,465 --> 01:33:12,425
Make your depth 1,200 feet,

1544
01:33:12,467 --> 01:33:14,302
20 degrees down angle.

1545
01:33:14,344 --> 01:33:18,098
Diving officer, make your depth
1,200 feet, 20 degree down.

1546
01:33:18,139 --> 01:33:20,558
Full dive
on fair water planes.

1547
01:33:20,642 --> 01:33:22,560
Full dive on stern planes.

1548
01:33:22,644 --> 01:33:24,729
20 degree down.

1549
01:33:27,982 --> 01:33:31,111
Passing 450 feet,
going to 1,200.

1550
01:33:33,154 --> 01:33:34,239
500.

1551
01:33:36,533 --> 01:33:37,992
600.

1552
01:33:40,036 --> 01:33:41,329
700.

1553
01:33:46,084 --> 01:33:48,378
Did you get a good fix?

1554
01:33:48,461 --> 01:33:49,421
Yes, Captain.

1555
01:33:49,504 --> 01:33:50,505
Very well.

1556
01:33:50,588 --> 01:33:52,799
At ordered depth,
1,200 feet.

1557
01:33:57,012 --> 01:33:59,848
Sonar, Conn.
You got him, yet, Jonesy?

1558
01:33:59,889 --> 01:34:02,100
Very faint, sir. Hold on.

1559
01:34:02,183 --> 01:34:05,979
Yes, sir. Contact typhoon seven
on bearing 2-5-0.

1560
01:34:06,021 --> 01:34:07,981
Right where he's supposed to be.

1561
01:34:08,023 --> 01:34:10,483
Any sign he's alerted
to our presence?

1562
01:34:10,525 --> 01:34:12,027
No. Operating as before.

1563
01:34:12,068 --> 01:34:13,987
Come left 2-6-5.

1564
01:34:14,029 --> 01:34:16,990
Bring us up
behind him quietly, Tommy.

1565
01:34:17,032 --> 01:34:18,074
Aye, aye, Captain.

1566
01:34:18,158 --> 01:34:20,827
Diving officer,
make your depth 500 feet.

1567
01:34:30,045 --> 01:34:31,796
Captain, please listen to me.

1568
01:34:31,880 --> 01:34:33,340
Two minutes, that's all.

1569
01:34:34,758 --> 01:34:37,344
Plot, time to intercept his track.

1570
01:34:37,427 --> 01:34:39,220
Four minutes, Captain.

1571
01:34:39,304 --> 01:34:41,389
Very well, Mr. Ryan.

1572
01:34:41,473 --> 01:34:42,724
Two minutes.

1573
01:34:49,397 --> 01:34:53,651
It makes perfect sense.
Look at the situation...

1574
01:34:53,735 --> 01:34:56,321
Ramius intends to defect.
The Russians know this,

1575
01:34:56,404 --> 01:34:57,864
which is why they've
tried to sink him,

1576
01:34:57,947 --> 01:35:00,408
but they haven't been able to.

1577
01:35:00,492 --> 01:35:02,410
I have a firing solution.

1578
01:35:02,452 --> 01:35:04,871
The Russians
will stop at nothing

1579
01:35:04,913 --> 01:35:06,873
to prevent Ramius
from defecting.

1580
01:35:06,915 --> 01:35:09,042
They've invented
this story that he's crazy

1581
01:35:09,084 --> 01:35:11,544
because they need us
to sink him

1582
01:35:11,586 --> 01:35:13,505
before he can contact us.

1583
01:35:15,590 --> 01:35:17,676
Weapons control,
I want full safeties.

1584
01:35:17,759 --> 01:35:20,387
I don't want this fish
coming back.

1585
01:35:20,470 --> 01:35:21,554
Full safety.

1586
01:35:21,638 --> 01:35:23,807
Captain, I know this man.

1587
01:35:24,849 --> 01:35:27,435
Has he made any Crazy lvans?

1588
01:35:27,519 --> 01:35:28,937
What difference does that make?

1589
01:35:29,020 --> 01:35:31,606
- The next one will be to starboard.
- 'Cause his last was to port?

1590
01:35:31,690 --> 01:35:34,401
Because he goes to starboard
in the bottom half of the hour.

1591
01:35:39,489 --> 01:35:41,908
Flood tubes one and two.
Warm up the weapons.

1592
01:35:41,950 --> 01:35:45,662
Find a way to establish contact
without violating orders.

1593
01:35:45,745 --> 01:35:47,163
He wants to defect!

1594
01:35:47,247 --> 01:35:49,249
Mr. Thompson,
call Chief Watson

1595
01:35:49,290 --> 01:35:51,126
to the conn with his sidearm.

1596
01:35:55,463 --> 01:35:58,091
Conn, Sonar.
Signal to noise ratio's dropping.

1597
01:35:58,174 --> 01:36:00,260
Possible aspect change
in target.

1598
01:36:00,343 --> 01:36:03,304
Possible target zig
based on bearing rate.

1599
01:36:05,140 --> 01:36:06,558
Conn, Sonar. Crazy lvan!

1600
01:36:10,687 --> 01:36:11,771
Captain.

1601
01:36:13,023 --> 01:36:15,400
Captain, he's turning.

1602
01:36:17,986 --> 01:36:20,030
Which way is he turning?

1603
01:36:20,113 --> 01:36:21,656
To the starboard, sir.

1604
01:36:22,824 --> 01:36:25,201
Give the man a chance.

1605
01:36:26,703 --> 01:36:28,621
All back full.

1606
01:36:28,663 --> 01:36:30,123
Captain, say again.

1607
01:36:30,165 --> 01:36:32,167
I said all back full!

1608
01:36:32,208 --> 01:36:33,668
Back full. Aye, sir.

1609
01:36:33,710 --> 01:36:34,794
Engines back full.

1610
01:36:39,674 --> 01:36:41,760
We're cavitating.
He can hear us!

1611
01:36:41,843 --> 01:36:44,179
Conn, aye. All stop.

1612
01:36:44,262 --> 01:36:45,555
All stop, aye.

1613
01:36:45,638 --> 01:36:48,600
All right.
We just unzipped our fly.

1614
01:36:48,683 --> 01:36:51,144
Mr. Thompson,
open the outer doors.

1615
01:36:51,186 --> 01:36:52,645
Firing point procedures.

1616
01:36:52,687 --> 01:36:55,607
Now, if that bastard
so much as twitches,

1617
01:36:55,690 --> 01:36:58,485
I'm going to blow him
right to Mars.

1618
01:36:58,526 --> 01:37:00,570
All stop rudder amid ship.

1619
01:37:00,653 --> 01:37:02,072
What's going on?

1620
01:37:02,155 --> 01:37:03,740
Can you identify the contact?

1621
01:37:03,823 --> 01:37:06,242
American Los Angeles-class
attack submarine

1622
01:37:06,326 --> 01:37:07,744
bearing 0-1-5...

1623
01:37:07,827 --> 01:37:09,245
Captain,
sharp metallic transients.

1624
01:37:09,329 --> 01:37:13,333
He may be trying to open
his torpedo tube doors.

1625
01:37:13,416 --> 01:37:16,920
Outer doors are open
on tubes one and two.

1626
01:37:17,003 --> 01:37:18,421
We're ready to shoot.

1627
01:37:18,505 --> 01:37:21,383
Very well.
My orders are specific, Mr. Ryan.

1628
01:37:21,424 --> 01:37:23,426
He's opened his outer doors.

1629
01:37:23,510 --> 01:37:24,552
He's preparing to fire.

1630
01:37:24,636 --> 01:37:28,181
Flood tubes three and four
and plot a solution.

1631
01:37:28,223 --> 01:37:31,810
Captain, flood tubes three and four.
Plot solution.

1632
01:37:31,893 --> 01:37:34,479
Shall I open
the torpedo tube door, sir?

1633
01:37:36,231 --> 01:37:39,693
Captain,
shall I open outer doors?

1634
01:37:39,734 --> 01:37:41,069
No.

1635
01:37:42,779 --> 01:37:45,365
Lock the firing solution
into the computer.

1636
01:37:45,448 --> 01:37:48,743
Do not open the doors.

1637
01:37:56,084 --> 01:37:57,919
Aye, Captain.

1638
01:37:59,129 --> 01:38:01,131
Lock solution into computer.

1639
01:38:02,382 --> 01:38:04,676
Conn, Sonar.
Target's flooded his tubes.

1640
01:38:04,759 --> 01:38:07,220
Has he opened his outer doors?

1641
01:38:07,262 --> 01:38:09,723
Negative, Captain.
He's just sitting there.

1642
01:38:09,764 --> 01:38:10,890
Hold on.

1643
01:38:10,974 --> 01:38:14,060
Hull popping.
Target's coming shallow.

1644
01:38:14,144 --> 01:38:16,021
What's that mean?

1645
01:38:17,147 --> 01:38:19,441
That means he's
a very cool customer,

1646
01:38:19,524 --> 01:38:20,567
your Russian.

1647
01:38:20,650 --> 01:38:23,611
He knows we're here
and ready to shoot.

1648
01:38:23,695 --> 01:38:25,113
He's not provoking us.

1649
01:38:25,196 --> 01:38:27,115
He's heading to periscope depth

1650
01:38:27,157 --> 01:38:29,617
to see what's on the surface.

1651
01:38:29,659 --> 01:38:32,620
His course is 2-0-7, due west.

1652
01:38:32,662 --> 01:38:34,122
Bring us alongside him.

1653
01:38:34,205 --> 01:38:35,623
Aye, aye, Captain.

1654
01:38:35,707 --> 01:38:37,959
Sonar, Conn.
Report all contacts.

1655
01:38:38,043 --> 01:38:39,544
Conn, Sonar.

1656
01:38:39,627 --> 01:38:44,132
My only contact is typhoon seven,
bearing 1-9-5.

1657
01:38:44,215 --> 01:38:47,135
Very well. Fire control,
range to target?

1658
01:38:47,177 --> 01:38:48,762
Range is 300 yards.

1659
01:38:50,221 --> 01:38:52,140
Make your depth 65 feet.

1660
01:38:52,182 --> 01:38:55,143
Diving Officer,
make your depth 65 feet.

1661
01:38:55,185 --> 01:38:58,646
He wants to go up
and take a peek.

1662
01:38:58,688 --> 01:39:00,440
We'll play along.

1663
01:39:15,914 --> 01:39:17,374
Going to 45.

1664
01:39:17,457 --> 01:39:19,334
Going to 45.

1665
01:39:19,417 --> 01:39:20,752
Weapons status?

1666
01:39:20,835 --> 01:39:22,504
Weapons are on.

1667
01:39:22,587 --> 01:39:24,339
Tubes three and four
are flooded.

1668
01:39:24,422 --> 01:39:25,632
Outer doors are closed.

1669
01:39:25,715 --> 01:39:27,550
25 meters.

1670
01:39:27,634 --> 01:39:29,052
Depth 25 meters.

1671
01:39:29,135 --> 01:39:31,554
Mark this bearing.

1672
01:39:37,936 --> 01:39:41,606
You wanted to talk
to him, Mr. Ryan.

1673
01:39:41,690 --> 01:39:43,316
There he is.

1674
01:39:44,317 --> 01:39:46,277
What do you want to say?

1675
01:39:46,361 --> 01:39:48,738
Is he the only one looking?

1676
01:39:48,822 --> 01:39:49,739
Probably.

1677
01:39:49,823 --> 01:39:51,908
We'll have to chance that.

1678
01:39:53,660 --> 01:39:57,122
"U.S. told you intend
missile launch". Break.

1679
01:39:57,205 --> 01:39:58,581
"Do not approach U.S. coast

1680
01:39:58,665 --> 01:40:02,585
or you will be attacked".
Break.

1681
01:40:02,669 --> 01:40:06,131
"If intention is other...

1682
01:40:06,214 --> 01:40:07,632
will you discuss option?"

1683
01:40:07,716 --> 01:40:08,633
Break.

1684
01:40:08,717 --> 01:40:11,594
Can he acknowledge
with a single ping?

1685
01:40:11,678 --> 01:40:14,097
Yeah, he can.

1686
01:40:14,180 --> 01:40:16,266
The question is, will he?

1687
01:40:23,898 --> 01:40:26,901
Verify our range to target.

1688
01:40:26,985 --> 01:40:29,821
One ping only.

1689
01:40:30,864 --> 01:40:32,407
Aye, captain.

1690
01:40:39,039 --> 01:40:40,457
I'll be damned.

1691
01:40:40,540 --> 01:40:42,542
Now what?

1692
01:40:44,252 --> 01:40:45,795
All right.

1693
01:40:45,879 --> 01:40:48,631
"If defection..."

1694
01:40:51,885 --> 01:40:55,680
You plot course toward..."

1695
01:40:55,764 --> 01:40:56,848
The chart.

1696
01:40:57,891 --> 01:40:59,309
I need a chart.

1697
01:40:59,392 --> 01:41:01,686
No. No.

1698
01:41:01,770 --> 01:41:03,855
OK. Where the hell are we?

1699
01:41:03,938 --> 01:41:07,650
Someplace deep.
Someplace deep.

1700
01:41:07,734 --> 01:41:09,736
OK.

1701
01:41:09,778 --> 01:41:10,820
OK!

1702
01:41:10,904 --> 01:41:12,781
Send them this!

1703
01:41:16,076 --> 01:41:18,370
You out of your mind?

1704
01:41:18,453 --> 01:41:21,081
Just send it.

1705
01:41:23,792 --> 01:41:25,168
Tell me one thing.

1706
01:41:25,251 --> 01:41:29,172
How did you know he was
going to go to starboard?

1707
01:41:29,255 --> 01:41:31,216
I didn't.

1708
01:41:31,299 --> 01:41:35,261
I had a 50/50 chance.
I needed a break. Sorry.

1709
01:41:35,345 --> 01:41:37,430
That's all right, Mr. Ryan.

1710
01:41:37,472 --> 01:41:39,224
My Morse is so rusty

1711
01:41:39,307 --> 01:41:41,184
I may be sending dimensions

1712
01:41:41,267 --> 01:41:43,645
on Playmate of the Month.

1713
01:42:03,331 --> 01:42:06,001
Reverify our range to target.

1714
01:42:07,669 --> 01:42:09,254
One ping only.

1715
01:42:09,337 --> 01:42:10,672
Captain, I... I... I just...

1716
01:42:10,755 --> 01:42:13,174
Give me a ping, Vasily.

1717
01:42:13,258 --> 01:42:16,428
One ping only, please.

1718
01:42:19,180 --> 01:42:20,390
Aye, Captain.

1719
01:42:24,185 --> 01:42:26,688
What the hell is this about?

1720
01:42:26,771 --> 01:42:30,316
The Russians
want us to sink her.

1721
01:42:31,693 --> 01:42:33,778
We might have to do that.

1722
01:42:41,536 --> 01:42:44,622
We must give this American
a wide berth.

1723
01:42:47,208 --> 01:42:48,960
Let us turn south.

1724
01:42:59,220 --> 01:43:00,388
Mr. Kamarov.

1725
01:43:02,724 --> 01:43:05,643
Plot a new course due south.

1726
01:43:05,727 --> 01:43:07,645
Yes, sir.

1727
01:43:07,729 --> 01:43:09,731
New course 1-8-0.

1728
01:43:42,764 --> 01:43:44,224
It's the reactor!

1729
01:43:44,265 --> 01:43:46,309
Shut down the reactor!

1730
01:43:48,770 --> 01:43:50,230
Seal the ventilation system!

1731
01:43:50,271 --> 01:43:52,232
The ventilation controls
have failed!

1732
01:43:52,273 --> 01:43:53,233
Everybody out.

1733
01:43:53,274 --> 01:43:55,402
Clear the compartment!

1734
01:43:56,653 --> 01:43:58,113
Out!

1735
01:44:07,789 --> 01:44:09,457
Ventilation system,
shut it down now.

1736
01:44:09,541 --> 01:44:11,084
They don't respond.

1737
01:44:11,167 --> 01:44:12,585
The controls don't respond!

1738
01:44:12,627 --> 01:44:14,629
Bypass it.
Seal it manually!

1739
01:44:14,671 --> 01:44:16,673
Move! Shut it off.

1740
01:44:17,674 --> 01:44:19,217
Melekhin, say again.

1741
01:44:26,474 --> 01:44:29,436
Suffered a leak
in the primary coolant tube,

1742
01:44:29,477 --> 01:44:30,937
so shut down the reactor,

1743
01:44:30,979 --> 01:44:33,565
but the ventilation circuits
have been disabled.

1744
01:44:33,648 --> 01:44:35,483
The coolant appears
highly contaminated.

1745
01:44:35,567 --> 01:44:37,527
I knew there was damage

1746
01:44:37,610 --> 01:44:39,487
when the caterpillar went down.

1747
01:44:39,571 --> 01:44:41,990
Radiation alarms activating
in all compartments.

1748
01:44:42,073 --> 01:44:43,491
Engineering
is heavily contaminated,

1749
01:44:43,575 --> 01:44:44,909
and radiation's moving forward.

1750
01:44:44,993 --> 01:44:46,411
Get to periscope depth.

1751
01:44:46,494 --> 01:44:47,787
Ventilate with outside air.

1752
01:44:47,829 --> 01:44:49,247
Aye, Captain.
Periscope depth!

1753
01:44:49,330 --> 01:44:50,749
Prepare to ventilate!

1754
01:44:50,832 --> 01:44:52,208
Ventilating won't do anything.

1755
01:44:52,292 --> 01:44:54,169
We've got to get them off.

1756
01:44:54,252 --> 01:44:55,670
What's the battery condition?

1757
01:44:55,712 --> 01:44:58,173
Four hours.
Six, if we're cautious.

1758
01:44:58,256 --> 01:44:59,674
Engage battery system.

1759
01:44:59,716 --> 01:45:02,427
We've got a level one
radiation leak.

1760
01:45:02,510 --> 01:45:04,971
Every surface of this
ship's contaminated.

1761
01:45:05,013 --> 01:45:07,932
We've got to get the men off.

1762
01:45:08,016 --> 01:45:09,934
Sir, we have been sabotaged!

1763
01:45:10,018 --> 01:45:12,479
Who said anything
about sabotage?

1764
01:45:12,520 --> 01:45:13,938
Captain!

1765
01:45:14,022 --> 01:45:17,108
Sir, I'm afraid
the doctor is right.

1766
01:45:22,697 --> 01:45:24,115
Very well.

1767
01:45:24,199 --> 01:45:27,118
Surface. We'll evacuate
the men to the deck.

1768
01:45:27,202 --> 01:45:28,119
Aye, Captain.

1769
01:45:28,203 --> 01:45:29,621
Surface the ship!

1770
01:45:29,704 --> 01:45:32,665
Prepare to muster all personnel
to escape hatches.

1771
01:45:32,707 --> 01:45:34,167
Break out the rafts.

1772
01:45:34,209 --> 01:45:36,169
Lash them to the deck.

1773
01:45:36,211 --> 01:45:40,173
We'll use them as shelters
until the fleet arrives.

1774
01:46:22,298 --> 01:46:24,759
Make sure the count
is accurate, Doctor.

1775
01:46:24,801 --> 01:46:27,679
We must get the entire crew out.

1776
01:46:27,762 --> 01:46:29,139
Yes, sir.

1777
01:46:29,222 --> 01:46:32,559
Master-At-Arms reports rafts secure
and evacuation proceeding.

1778
01:46:32,600 --> 01:46:33,685
Very good.

1779
01:46:40,525 --> 01:46:42,444
We'll rotate through the conn.

1780
01:46:42,527 --> 01:46:46,448
No officer will spend
more than 20 minutes below deck.

1781
01:46:46,531 --> 01:46:47,949
Will that be satisfactory?

1782
01:46:48,033 --> 01:46:49,075
Completely, Captain.

1783
01:46:49,159 --> 01:46:50,368
Surface contact!

1784
01:46:50,452 --> 01:46:51,953
2-7-0.

1785
01:46:52,037 --> 01:46:54,831
6 miles and closing fast.

1786
01:46:54,914 --> 01:46:56,082
It's a warship.

1787
01:46:56,166 --> 01:46:57,125
What? Here?

1788
01:46:57,208 --> 01:46:58,626
Can you identify it?

1789
01:46:58,668 --> 01:47:00,128
It's a frigate,

1790
01:47:00,170 --> 01:47:01,796
U.S., probably,

1791
01:47:01,880 --> 01:47:03,798
Perry class.
He's signaling.

1792
01:47:04,674 --> 01:47:08,303
"Red October.
Red October.

1793
01:47:08,386 --> 01:47:10,180
Halt and stay where you are.

1794
01:47:10,263 --> 01:47:12,057
Do not attempt to submerge,

1795
01:47:12,140 --> 01:47:14,309
or you will be fired upon".

1796
01:47:16,519 --> 01:47:19,981
Captain, I think
he means to board us.

1797
01:47:25,653 --> 01:47:28,073
You will go with the crew.

1798
01:47:28,156 --> 01:47:31,076
The officers and I will submerge beneath you

1799
01:47:31,159 --> 01:47:32,660
and scuttle the ship.

1800
01:47:34,204 --> 01:47:37,290
You'll receive the order of Lenin
for this, Captain.

1801
01:47:46,925 --> 01:47:48,843
We're going to cast off.

1802
01:47:48,927 --> 01:47:52,013
The captain's going
to scuttle the ship.

1803
01:47:57,435 --> 01:47:58,770
He's going down, sir.

1804
01:47:58,853 --> 01:48:00,647
Put a shot across his bow.

1805
01:48:00,689 --> 01:48:02,148
Come right at 1-8-0.

1806
01:48:32,679 --> 01:48:35,056
Seahawk One,
this is Bravo Command.

1807
01:48:53,616 --> 01:48:54,868
She's ready.

1808
01:48:57,037 --> 01:48:59,414
Seahawk One,
release on my command.

1809
01:48:59,497 --> 01:49:02,667
Drop now...now...now.

1810
01:49:10,759 --> 01:49:13,011
Range to target...
900 yards.

1811
01:49:14,637 --> 01:49:15,847
800 yards.

1812
01:49:18,558 --> 01:49:19,893
700 yards.

1813
01:49:21,603 --> 01:49:23,521
600 yards.

1814
01:49:28,401 --> 01:49:29,652
500 yards.

1815
01:49:32,405 --> 01:49:34,074
400 yards.

1816
01:49:36,117 --> 01:49:37,827
300 yards.

1817
01:49:40,622 --> 01:49:42,332
The torpedo is detonated.

1818
01:49:43,750 --> 01:49:46,127
Now, understand, Commander,

1819
01:49:46,169 --> 01:49:48,588
that torpedo
did not self-destruct.

1820
01:49:48,630 --> 01:49:51,091
You heard it hit the hull.

1821
01:49:51,132 --> 01:49:54,344
And I...was never here.

1822
01:49:54,427 --> 01:49:56,888
Contact Dallas.
Give them the go.

1823
01:49:56,971 --> 01:49:59,516
Advise Dallas.
Get the DSRV moving.

1824
01:49:59,599 --> 01:50:01,559
Dallas, this is Rueben James.

1825
01:50:07,982 --> 01:50:09,776
There are about
two million things

1826
01:50:09,818 --> 01:50:12,612
that can still go wrong
with this stunt.

1827
01:50:12,654 --> 01:50:15,073
Central lntelligence Agency.

1828
01:50:15,156 --> 01:50:17,659
Now, there's
a contradiction in terms.

1829
01:50:19,369 --> 01:50:21,162
How's the coffee, Ryan?

1830
01:50:22,372 --> 01:50:24,332
Dallas, this is the Mystic.

1831
01:50:24,416 --> 01:50:25,834
Request clearance for launch.

1832
01:50:25,917 --> 01:50:28,503
Mystic, Dallas.
You're cleared for launch.

1833
01:50:29,504 --> 01:50:30,964
Hatch is secured.

1834
01:50:31,006 --> 01:50:33,425
Life support, normal.
Prelaunch checks complete.

1835
01:50:33,508 --> 01:50:34,926
We're ready to launch.

1836
01:50:35,010 --> 01:50:35,969
Aye.

1837
01:50:36,052 --> 01:50:36,970
Flood the skirt.

1838
01:50:37,012 --> 01:50:38,346
Flood the skirt. Aye.

1839
01:50:38,430 --> 01:50:40,306
Skirt's filled and equalized.

1840
01:50:40,348 --> 01:50:42,809
Soft seal.
Ready to lift off.

1841
01:50:42,851 --> 01:50:44,936
OK. Let's do it.

1842
01:50:46,688 --> 01:50:48,606
We're off.

1843
01:50:48,690 --> 01:50:50,483
Thrusting port.

1844
01:51:11,254 --> 01:51:13,715
600 yards and closing.

1845
01:51:13,757 --> 01:51:16,468
Steer right to course 0-7-5.

1846
01:51:16,551 --> 01:51:17,469
400.

1847
01:51:21,473 --> 01:51:23,391
200 yards.

1848
01:51:25,810 --> 01:51:27,562
100 yards.

1849
01:51:34,944 --> 01:51:36,529
Hit the lights.

1850
01:51:49,751 --> 01:51:53,213
Jesus, that's a big sucker.

1851
01:52:08,687 --> 01:52:10,855
OK, we're in position.

1852
01:52:10,939 --> 01:52:13,608
You can open the hatch.

1853
01:52:13,692 --> 01:52:15,235
Hold it a second.

1854
01:52:15,276 --> 01:52:16,361
Jonesy.

1855
01:52:18,446 --> 01:52:19,823
Mr. Ryan.

1856
01:52:23,118 --> 01:52:24,911
He's defecting.

1857
01:52:25,870 --> 01:52:28,289
And he can't change his mind?

1858
01:52:28,331 --> 01:52:30,792
He's not going
to change his mind.

1859
01:52:30,875 --> 01:52:34,087
You willing
to bet your life on that?

1860
01:52:53,356 --> 01:52:55,900
Uh, sir, could you
hand me that hammer?

1861
01:52:55,984 --> 01:52:57,068
Yeah.

1862
01:53:19,549 --> 01:53:20,800
Americans.

1863
01:55:29,554 --> 01:55:31,097
What's so funny?

1864
01:55:32,640 --> 01:55:35,143
Well, the captain
seems to think

1865
01:55:35,185 --> 01:55:37,270
you're some sort of cowboy.

1866
01:55:51,493 --> 01:55:52,702
It is.

1867
01:55:54,996 --> 01:55:56,456
I doubt you'd remember,

1868
01:55:56,498 --> 01:55:59,959
but we met once
at the consulate in Leningrad.

1869
01:56:00,001 --> 01:56:02,212
Along with your wife.

1870
01:56:02,295 --> 01:56:03,838
I'm very sorry.

1871
01:56:06,383 --> 01:56:11,346
What gives you the right
to fire on my ship?

1872
01:56:11,388 --> 01:56:14,182
Your signal said nothing
of a torpedo.

1873
01:56:14,265 --> 01:56:15,433
Ryan.

1874
01:56:16,851 --> 01:56:20,188
It was necessary to maintain
the illusion for your crew.

1875
01:56:20,271 --> 01:56:22,691
My crew are being rescued, yes?

1876
01:56:22,774 --> 01:56:24,484
As we speak.

1877
01:56:28,488 --> 01:56:30,490
You sent the signal.

1878
01:56:30,573 --> 01:56:32,117
That's correct, sir.

1879
01:56:33,868 --> 01:56:38,581
Then how did you know
our reactor accident was false?

1880
01:56:40,041 --> 01:56:43,461
Well, that was a guess,
but it seemed logical.

1881
01:56:49,801 --> 01:56:51,177
Very well.

1882
01:56:54,973 --> 01:56:57,517
I present you

1883
01:56:57,600 --> 01:57:00,645
the ballistic missile
submarine Red October.

1884
01:57:04,858 --> 01:57:08,528
My officers and I
request asylum...

1885
01:57:09,612 --> 01:57:12,198
in the United States
of America.

1886
01:57:14,075 --> 01:57:16,161
It's a pleasure, sir.

1887
01:57:16,244 --> 01:57:18,371
Bart Mancuso, USS Dallas.

1888
01:57:21,374 --> 01:57:24,377
Torpedo. The Americans
are shooting at us again.

1889
01:57:24,419 --> 01:57:26,379
The pitch is too high.

1890
01:57:26,421 --> 01:57:28,548
The torpedo's Russian.

1891
01:57:28,590 --> 01:57:31,259
Where the hell
did it come from?

1892
01:57:31,343 --> 01:57:33,553
Conn, Sonar.
New contact. Sierra 4-1.

1893
01:57:33,595 --> 01:57:35,263
Alfa class Soviet submarine.

1894
01:57:38,850 --> 01:57:40,769
Why don't I have a detonation?

1895
01:57:40,810 --> 01:57:44,105
The weapon enabled
on the far side of the target.

1896
01:57:44,189 --> 01:57:46,107
It passed Red October
before arming.

1897
01:57:46,191 --> 01:57:49,611
Fire again with the right settings
and reload both tubes.

1898
01:57:49,652 --> 01:57:52,489
Get me power.
Get that damn thing off my boat.

1899
01:57:52,572 --> 01:57:53,531
It's off.

1900
01:57:54,741 --> 01:57:57,369
I think somebody
shot a torpedo at us.

1901
01:57:57,452 --> 01:57:59,704
No shit, Buckwheat.
Get out of here.

1902
01:57:59,788 --> 01:58:00,830
Where am I...

1903
01:58:15,136 --> 01:58:17,806
Borodin, fire control.

1904
01:58:18,848 --> 01:58:19,891
Uh, Ryan.

1905
01:58:19,974 --> 01:58:20,975
Sit here.

1906
01:58:21,059 --> 01:58:24,145
I'm not a naval officer.
I'm with the CIA.

1907
01:58:24,229 --> 01:58:25,146
CIA?

1908
01:58:25,188 --> 01:58:26,648
I'm not an agent.

1909
01:58:26,731 --> 01:58:29,150
I just write books
for the CIA.

1910
01:58:29,234 --> 01:58:30,694
Whatever. Sit down.

1911
01:58:30,777 --> 01:58:32,821
And do exactly what I tell you.

1912
01:58:32,904 --> 01:58:34,114
DSRVs away.

1913
01:58:34,155 --> 01:58:36,449
There's a Russian alfa
8,000 yards to starboard.

1914
01:58:36,533 --> 01:58:38,284
I think it's the Konovalov.

1915
01:58:38,368 --> 01:58:39,828
Increase the flank.

1916
01:58:39,911 --> 01:58:41,955
Increase the flank.

1917
01:58:41,996 --> 01:58:42,914
How?

1918
01:58:44,082 --> 01:58:46,835
That knob. Turn right full.

1919
01:58:48,169 --> 01:58:50,338
All ahead flank.
Right full rudder.

1920
01:58:50,422 --> 01:58:51,881
He's shooting again, sir.

1921
01:58:51,965 --> 01:58:53,383
Go to battle stations.

1922
01:58:53,466 --> 01:58:54,342
Battle stations.

1923
01:58:54,426 --> 01:58:57,345
Chief of Watch,
man battle stations.

1924
01:59:00,682 --> 01:59:02,642
Torpedo in the water.

1925
01:59:02,684 --> 01:59:03,893
Bearing 3-1-5.

1926
01:59:03,977 --> 01:59:05,270
Make range 7,000 yards.

1927
01:59:05,353 --> 01:59:07,689
Steer right 3-1-5.

1928
01:59:07,772 --> 01:59:09,816
That's heading
into the torpedo.

1929
01:59:09,899 --> 01:59:11,860
Steady,
bearing still 3-1-5.

1930
01:59:11,943 --> 01:59:13,987
Estimated range...
6,000 yards.

1931
01:59:14,070 --> 01:59:15,613
Still 3-1-5.

1932
01:59:15,697 --> 01:59:18,366
Steer right
till this reads 3-1-5.

1933
01:59:18,450 --> 01:59:21,745
No, that's wrong.
Ryan, don't turn that goddamn wheel.

1934
01:59:23,079 --> 01:59:25,040
3-1-5.

1935
01:59:35,675 --> 01:59:38,053
You're heading straight
into that torpedo.

1936
01:59:39,346 --> 01:59:40,722
Yes.

1937
01:59:40,764 --> 01:59:42,015
Doing what?

1938
01:59:42,057 --> 01:59:43,933
He's turned
into the torpedo's path.

1939
01:59:44,017 --> 01:59:45,685
What?

1940
01:59:45,769 --> 01:59:48,730
Red October's turned directly
into the torpedo's path, sir.

1941
01:59:48,813 --> 01:59:50,148
Mother of God.

1942
01:59:50,231 --> 01:59:52,609
Torpedo steady,
bearing 3-1-5.

1943
01:59:52,692 --> 01:59:54,110
Range...5,000 yards.

1944
01:59:54,194 --> 01:59:55,612
Melekhin, more speed.

1945
02:00:00,075 --> 02:00:02,786
Estimate range 3,000 yards.

1946
02:00:02,869 --> 02:00:04,871
Closing awfully fast.

1947
02:00:11,419 --> 02:00:13,880
What's he trying to do,
kill himself?

1948
02:00:13,963 --> 02:00:16,925
We have a firing solution
on the Russian alfa.

1949
02:00:17,008 --> 02:00:18,426
Can we shoot back?

1950
02:00:18,468 --> 02:00:19,928
They didn't shoot at us.

1951
02:00:20,011 --> 02:00:22,430
I can't attack a Soviet submarine
without authorization.

1952
02:00:25,100 --> 02:00:27,227
Torpedo bearing steady at 3-1-5.

1953
02:00:27,310 --> 02:00:29,938
Best range... 900 yards.

1954
02:00:31,189 --> 02:00:34,609
Torpedo impact...
20 seconds.

1955
02:00:36,361 --> 02:00:37,529
What books?

1956
02:00:39,114 --> 02:00:40,407
Pardon me.

1957
02:00:40,490 --> 02:00:42,075
What books did you write?

1958
02:00:43,827 --> 02:00:48,039
I wrote a biography on Admiral Halsey
called The Fighting Sailor,

1959
02:00:48,123 --> 02:00:49,791
about naval combat tactics.

1960
02:00:49,874 --> 02:00:51,376
I know this book.

1961
02:00:51,459 --> 02:00:52,377
Torpedo impact...

1962
02:00:52,460 --> 02:00:54,879
Your conclusions
were all wrong, Ryan.

1963
02:00:54,963 --> 02:00:55,922
10 seconds.

1964
02:00:55,964 --> 02:00:58,174
Halsey acted stupidly.

1965
02:00:58,258 --> 02:00:59,509
9...

1966
02:00:59,592 --> 02:01:00,677
8...

1967
02:01:00,760 --> 02:01:02,387
7...

1968
02:01:02,470 --> 02:01:03,513
6...

1969
02:01:03,596 --> 02:01:04,556
5...

1970
02:01:04,639 --> 02:01:06,182
4...

1971
02:01:06,266 --> 02:01:07,475
3...

1972
02:01:07,559 --> 02:01:08,476
2...

1973
02:01:08,518 --> 02:01:10,228
Torpedo impact...

1974
02:01:11,354 --> 02:01:12,772
now.

1975
02:01:21,823 --> 02:01:24,159
I'll be damned.

1976
02:01:25,618 --> 02:01:27,120
What happened?

1977
02:01:27,203 --> 02:01:29,664
Combat tactics, Mr. Ryan.

1978
02:01:29,748 --> 02:01:31,666
By turning into the torpedo,

1979
02:01:31,750 --> 02:01:33,668
the captain closed the distance

1980
02:01:33,752 --> 02:01:36,171
before it could arm itself.

1981
02:01:36,212 --> 02:01:38,048
So that's it?

1982
02:01:38,131 --> 02:01:39,257
Not quite.

1983
02:01:39,341 --> 02:01:40,800
Right now, Captain Tupolev

1984
02:01:40,884 --> 02:01:44,304
is removing the safety
features on all his weapons.

1985
02:01:44,346 --> 02:01:47,307
He won't make
the same mistake twice.

1986
02:01:47,349 --> 02:01:50,143
Vasily, have you
plotted a solution yet?

1987
02:01:50,185 --> 02:01:52,687
Aye, Captain.
It's a little rough, but I...

1988
02:01:59,069 --> 02:02:00,153
Gunfire?

1989
02:02:00,236 --> 02:02:02,322
That's what it sounded like, sir.

1990
02:02:02,405 --> 02:02:03,990
Won't change his mind?

1991
02:02:04,032 --> 02:02:05,492
It's one of the crew.

1992
02:02:05,533 --> 02:02:08,453
Well, whoever he is,
he's having second thoughts.

1993
02:02:26,054 --> 02:02:29,182
I would like
to have seen Montana.

1994
02:02:42,320 --> 02:02:45,323
Captain, he stumbled forward
into the missile bay.

1995
02:02:45,407 --> 02:02:47,409
There's a silo hatch warning...

1996
02:02:47,492 --> 02:02:48,952
port side, number 20.

1997
02:02:49,035 --> 02:02:50,537
He can't launch a missile.

1998
02:02:50,620 --> 02:02:53,873
No, but he can blow one up.

1999
02:02:55,458 --> 02:02:56,918
Captain, take the conn.

2000
02:02:56,960 --> 02:02:58,712
Fire control's blasted to hell.

2001
02:02:58,795 --> 02:03:01,131
Then get behind him
and stay there.

2002
02:03:01,214 --> 02:03:02,173
Captain, wait.

2003
02:03:05,802 --> 02:03:08,054
You may need this, sir.

2004
02:03:11,474 --> 02:03:12,726
Thank you.

2005
02:03:15,603 --> 02:03:18,565
Don't just stand there, Ryan.
Go with him!

2006
02:03:18,606 --> 02:03:20,025
You, you speak English?

2007
02:03:20,108 --> 02:03:21,067
Yes, sir.

2008
02:03:21,151 --> 02:03:22,902
Get your butt over here.

2009
02:03:22,986 --> 02:03:25,572
Captain, torpedo
broke off on impact.

2010
02:03:25,655 --> 02:03:27,907
Set the safety ranges
for zero.

2011
02:03:27,949 --> 02:03:29,743
I cannot go any faster.

2012
02:03:29,826 --> 02:03:31,244
We must get him

2013
02:03:31,286 --> 02:03:33,288
before he gets to
the ignition circuits.

2014
02:03:41,463 --> 02:03:44,924
What happens if he gets
to that ignition circuitry?

2015
02:03:44,966 --> 02:03:47,093
He can incinerate the ship.

2016
02:03:55,226 --> 02:03:59,064
Is that door
the only way out of here?

2017
02:03:59,147 --> 02:04:00,315
Yes.

2018
02:04:00,398 --> 02:04:02,776
Don't let him get past you.

2019
02:04:03,902 --> 02:04:05,612
Hey, Ryan.

2020
02:04:05,695 --> 02:04:09,074
Be careful
what you shoot at, hmm?

2021
02:04:09,157 --> 02:04:10,575
Most things in here

2022
02:04:10,658 --> 02:04:13,244
don't react too well
to bullets.

2023
02:04:16,122 --> 02:04:17,374
Right.

2024
02:04:35,975 --> 02:04:39,062
I have to be careful
what I shoot at?

2025
02:04:47,195 --> 02:04:49,072
Safety ranges set to zero.

2026
02:04:49,155 --> 02:04:52,117
Sonar, give me the bearing
for Red October.

2027
02:04:52,200 --> 02:04:53,618
She's positioned behind us.

2028
02:04:53,702 --> 02:04:55,078
Right full rudder!

2029
02:04:55,161 --> 02:04:57,247
I'll shake the man loose.

2030
02:05:03,461 --> 02:05:04,504
He's going deep.

2031
02:05:04,587 --> 02:05:06,089
30 degree down angle!

2032
02:05:06,172 --> 02:05:08,925
All the countermeasures
were over there, right?

2033
02:05:09,009 --> 02:05:09,884
Very well.

2034
02:05:35,410 --> 02:05:37,370
Admiral,
the alfa's too quick.

2035
02:05:37,412 --> 02:05:39,497
It's only a matter of time.

2036
02:05:39,581 --> 02:05:42,208
"Ryan, some things in here

2037
02:05:42,292 --> 02:05:44,169
don't react well to bullets".

2038
02:05:44,252 --> 02:05:45,086
Yeah.

2039
02:05:45,170 --> 02:05:46,463
Like me.

2040
02:05:46,546 --> 02:05:49,132
I don't react well to bullets.

2041
02:06:05,273 --> 02:06:06,232
Aah!

2042
02:06:07,609 --> 02:06:08,735
Where's he now?

2043
02:06:08,777 --> 02:06:09,694
Close, sir.

2044
02:06:13,406 --> 02:06:15,450
900 meters, directly ahead.

2045
02:06:15,533 --> 02:06:16,576
Got him.

2046
02:06:16,618 --> 02:06:18,119
Match bearings and fire.

2047
02:06:18,161 --> 02:06:19,621
We are too close!

2048
02:06:19,662 --> 02:06:21,581
Don't argue.
Launch the weapon.

2049
02:06:21,623 --> 02:06:24,417
The torpedoes are armed
in the tubes! You cannot...

2050
02:06:24,501 --> 02:06:25,710
Do it now!

2051
02:06:27,128 --> 02:06:30,715
Another torpedo. It went active
the moment it was launched.

2052
02:06:30,799 --> 02:06:34,135
Left full rudder.
Ease off on your bow planes.

2053
02:06:34,219 --> 02:06:35,637
I think he's got us.

2054
02:06:45,397 --> 02:06:47,482
Way to go, Dallas!

2055
02:06:52,696 --> 02:06:54,280
Torpedo is in acquisition.

2056
02:06:54,322 --> 02:06:56,282
Range 500 yards and closing.

2057
02:06:56,324 --> 02:06:58,326
You've done it, Mr. Thompson!

2058
02:06:58,368 --> 02:06:59,786
I hope this works.

2059
02:06:59,828 --> 02:07:02,288
Chief, put us on the roof.

2060
02:07:02,330 --> 02:07:05,959
Countermeasure station,
on my mark. 5, 4...

2061
02:07:08,795 --> 02:07:10,630
1. Release countermeasures.

2062
02:07:10,714 --> 02:07:11,965
Emergency blow!

2063
02:07:12,007 --> 02:07:13,842
Full rise. Fair water plane.

2064
02:07:19,514 --> 02:07:20,807
600 feet.

2065
02:07:20,849 --> 02:07:22,559
550...

2066
02:07:22,642 --> 02:07:24,644
500 feet.

2067
02:07:24,728 --> 02:07:25,645
450...

2068
02:07:25,687 --> 02:07:28,023
Come on, big D. Fly.

2069
02:07:47,167 --> 02:07:48,585
That torpedo's still active.

2070
02:07:48,668 --> 02:07:50,712
It's searching
for another target.

2071
02:08:15,945 --> 02:08:18,031
A goddamn cook!

2072
02:08:46,726 --> 02:08:49,187
How close is that alfa, Jonesy?

2073
02:08:49,270 --> 02:08:50,772
1,000 yards, going to port.

2074
02:08:50,855 --> 02:08:52,899
Reverse your turn.

2075
02:08:58,154 --> 02:09:00,615
We're turning straight at him.

2076
02:09:00,657 --> 02:09:02,450
Torpedo's still on our tail.

2077
02:09:06,371 --> 02:09:08,915
The hard part
about playing chicken

2078
02:09:08,957 --> 02:09:11,126
is knowing when to flinch.

2079
02:09:16,589 --> 02:09:18,049
Collision in 400 yards.

2080
02:09:18,133 --> 02:09:19,300
350...

2081
02:09:19,384 --> 02:09:20,468
300...

2082
02:09:20,552 --> 02:09:22,220
Captain!

2083
02:09:22,303 --> 02:09:25,223
Right full rudder,
30 degree down angle.

2084
02:09:25,306 --> 02:09:26,599
Aye, sir.

2085
02:09:26,641 --> 02:09:28,643
This one's going to be close.

2086
02:09:32,981 --> 02:09:34,399
Torpedo dead ahead!

2087
02:09:34,482 --> 02:09:36,776
You arrogant ass.
You've killed us.

2088
02:10:04,846 --> 02:10:07,307
We have ascertained
the Red October's

2089
02:10:07,390 --> 02:10:09,476
final position, but...

2090
02:10:09,559 --> 02:10:11,644
given the depth of the water

2091
02:10:11,686 --> 02:10:13,646
and the fact that the wreckage

2092
02:10:13,688 --> 02:10:15,690
spreads across a wide area,

2093
02:10:15,774 --> 02:10:18,651
it'll be some time before
anything's recovered.

2094
02:10:18,693 --> 02:10:21,654
However, your people
are interviewing the crew now

2095
02:10:21,696 --> 02:10:24,199
and making arrangements
for their return.

2096
02:10:24,282 --> 02:10:27,202
This has been a terrible tragedy,
Mr. Ambassador.

2097
02:10:27,243 --> 02:10:29,204
And I can only stress

2098
02:10:29,245 --> 02:10:32,207
that if you'd come to us earlier

2099
02:10:32,248 --> 02:10:34,209
it might have been avoided.

2100
02:10:34,250 --> 02:10:37,212
I appreciate your candor
in the matter.

2101
02:10:37,253 --> 02:10:39,756
And I yours, Andrei.

2102
02:10:43,301 --> 02:10:44,719
Perhaps in the future

2103
02:10:44,761 --> 02:10:47,847
technology will allow
a more thorough investigation

2104
02:10:47,889 --> 02:10:49,182
of the wreckage.

2105
02:10:49,224 --> 02:10:51,226
Perhaps.

2106
02:10:57,190 --> 02:10:59,317
There is another matter...

2107
02:10:59,401 --> 02:11:01,486
one that I'm reluctant to...

2108
02:11:01,569 --> 02:11:03,738
Please.

2109
02:11:04,781 --> 02:11:07,242
One of our submarines...
an alfa...

2110
02:11:07,325 --> 02:11:11,538
was last reported in the area
of the Grand Banks.

2111
02:11:11,621 --> 02:11:15,166
We have not heard
from her for some time.

2112
02:11:17,752 --> 02:11:19,212
Andrei.

2113
02:11:20,255 --> 02:11:22,632
You've lost another submarine?

2114
02:11:37,105 --> 02:11:41,443
All this way to hide
a submarine in a river.

2115
02:11:41,526 --> 02:11:44,738
We're 100 miles
from the nearest naval base,

2116
02:11:44,821 --> 02:11:47,907
the last place
that satellites will ever look.

2117
02:11:50,035 --> 02:11:52,078
I grew up around here.

2118
02:11:53,997 --> 02:11:56,499
My grandfather taught me to fish

2119
02:11:56,583 --> 02:11:59,085
off that island right over there.

2120
02:12:02,464 --> 02:12:03,923
There's one question

2121
02:12:03,965 --> 02:12:06,092
you haven't asked me yet.

2122
02:12:07,802 --> 02:12:09,888
Why?

2123
02:12:11,473 --> 02:12:14,893
Well, I figured you would tell me
when you were ready.

2124
02:12:16,686 --> 02:12:19,147
Now, there are
those who believe

2125
02:12:19,230 --> 02:12:22,150
we should attack
the United States first,

2126
02:12:22,192 --> 02:12:24,944
settle everything
in one moment.

2127
02:12:24,986 --> 02:12:27,989
Red October was built
for that purpose.

2128
02:12:28,031 --> 02:12:30,492
When the dust settles
from this,

2129
02:12:30,533 --> 02:12:33,495
there's going to be
hell to pay in Moscow.

2130
02:12:33,578 --> 02:12:34,662
Well, perhaps.

2131
02:12:34,746 --> 02:12:37,665
Maybe something good
will come from it.

2132
02:12:37,707 --> 02:12:40,168
A little revolution,
now and then,

2133
02:12:40,210 --> 02:12:43,296
is a healthy thing,
don't you think?

2134
02:12:49,427 --> 02:12:51,846
Do you still like to fish, Ryan?

2135
02:12:51,888 --> 02:12:53,515
Mm-hmm.

2136
02:12:54,599 --> 02:12:57,519
There's a river,
not unlike this one,

2137
02:12:57,560 --> 02:12:58,520
near Vilnius,

2138
02:12:58,603 --> 02:13:01,648
where my grandfather
taught me to fish.

2139
02:13:04,651 --> 02:13:09,364
"And the sea will grant
each man new hope

2140
02:13:09,447 --> 02:13:14,661
as sleep brings dreams of home".

2141
02:13:19,416 --> 02:13:21,334
Christopher Columbus.

2142
02:13:25,922 --> 02:13:28,508
Welcome to the New World, sir.

2143
02:13:38,184 --> 02:13:39,853
Everything all right, sir?
